// engine/level/scene_dsl.emp — the authored scene model (spec 2026-08-17 §2/§3).
//
// Pure comptime: constructors, capability fold, lowering to parallax_config/band_entry.
// GUARDS LIVE HERE: this module belongs in the use closure of every scene author, so
// its ensures evaluate on every authored scene (the preset.emp reachability lesson).
//
// THAT CLOSURE DOES NOT EXIST YET, AND THE COVERAGE BOUNDARY IS EXACTLY THIS: the file
// has PARSE + SCAN coverage today and ZERO body-elaboration coverage. Measured from both
// sides. A syntax error here fails the build (`error: expected an expression, found
// AndAnd`), so the file cannot rot into garbage unnoticed — but `return unknown_name +
// MISSING_CONST` inside an uncalled `pub comptime fn` builds GREEN with an identical CRC,
// and so does a module-level `ensure(1 == 0)`. So every ensure in this file, module-level
// pins included, is dead until something reaches it: Task 5's registry, or Task 7's
// permanent equivalence witness, whichever lands first. Nothing here may be trusted on
// the strength of a green build; every guard below was red-first verified through a
// temporary probe that forced elaboration from a placed module.
//
// Pure comptime — this module emits no ROM bytes.
module engine.level.scene_dsl

use engine.structs.{parallax_config}
use engine.parallax.{band_entry, band_record, band_ext, BAND_EXT_N, band_curve, BAND_CURVE_N, CURVE_FLAG_OP_BIT, CURVE_FLAG_ACTIVE_BIT, PLANE_B_SPAN, BAND_OP_BIT_A, BAND_OP_BIT_B}
use engine.constants.{MAX_PARALLAX_BANDS, PARALLAX_ANCHOR_NONE}
use engine.effects.raster_dsl.{RASTER_MAX_PATCH}

// ---- the inlined-literal pins (the call-site resolution rule) ----
// A comptime fn's free names resolve at the CALL SITE, and this module is NOT a
// COMPTIME_HELPERS member (sigil crates/sigil-harness/src/native.rs:1942 holds that
// list; scene_dsl is not on it), so nothing here is glob-injected. Names IMPORTED
// above therefore do NOT travel into a body a GAME module calls: spelled as constants
// inside scene()/scene_hdr(), MAX_PARALLAX_BANDS / PARALLAX_ANCHOR_NONE /
// RASTER_MAX_PATCH would resolve to nothing at a game call site — exactly the failure
// raster_dsl.emp:449-457 records, where an imported bound collapsed a range to empty
// and compose() emitted ZERO fires with no diagnostic. Every body below spells the
// NUMBER; these three pins hold the numbers to their authorities.
//
// Names DEFINED here (the CAP_*/TRANS_* consts, the enums, and every helper
// below) ARE safe to name in a body — but only because a scene author reaches them
// through a GLOB import. THAT IS A REQUIREMENT ON CALLERS: a scene module must
// `use engine.level.scene_dsl.*`, never a selective `use ...{scene, layer}`.
//
// THE TWO AXES FAIL VERY DIFFERENTLY, which is the whole reason bodies inline numbers
// but keep calling helpers by name. A selective import is LOUD on the function axis —
// one `unknown function` error per call site (measured twice, at 217 and 152 errors; the
// count is a property of the probe, the SHAPE is the fact), so that constraint enforces
// itself and no build can slip past it. It is SILENT on the constant axis: a name that resolves to
// nothing inside a body does not error, it degrades (raster_dsl.emp:449-457 — an
// imported bound collapsed a range to empty and compose() emitted zero fires). Loud is
// tolerable; silent is not. Hence: helpers by name (all `pub`, for this reason and no
// other), constants as literals held by the pins below.
//
// TWO SPECIES OF PIN, kept apart on purpose. These three watch constants owned by OTHER
// modules, where drift is a real event nobody editing this file would see:
ensure(MAX_PARALLAX_BANDS == 16, "scene_dsl's inlined band ceiling (16) drifted from MAX_PARALLAX_BANDS — every literal 16 governed by this pin is listed in the constant's own comment block in engine/system/constants.emp; move them together or the fixed-size layer arrays and the ceiling disagree")
ensure(PARALLAX_ANCHOR_NONE == $FF, "scene_dsl's inlined no-anchor sentinel ($FF) drifted from PARALLAX_ANCHOR_NONE")
ensure(RASTER_MAX_PATCH == 4, "scene_dsl's inlined patch-channel width (4) drifted from RASTER_MAX_PATCH")
// P3 Task 7 added three more of the same species. PLANE_B_SPAN and the two op bits are
// parallax's; LAYER_WORLD_Y_MAX is defined here because it is a property of the AUTHORED
// coordinate space, not of the walker.
ensure(PLANE_B_SPAN == 512, "scene_dsl's inlined Plane-B span (512) drifted from engine.parallax.PLANE_B_SPAN — scene_plane_line()'s range guard and Step 4a's rotation modulus must be the same number")
ensure(BAND_OP_BIT_A == 0 && BAND_OP_BIT_B == 1, "scene_dsl's inlined band factor-op bit positions (0, 1) drifted from engine.parallax.BAND_OP_BIT_A/B — scene_band() packs the two flags into band_factor_ops and Decode_Factor_A/B btst them back out")
// P3 Task 10 adds two of the same species. scene_band() spells the curve tail's flag bits
// as 1 and 2 for the reason above — a name imported HERE resolves to nothing inside a body
// a GAME module elaborates — and these hold those two literals to parallax's own bit
// numbers, which Decode_Factor_Curve and the fill's band hoist btst back out.
ensure(CURVE_FLAG_OP_BIT == 0 && CURVE_FLAG_ACTIVE_BIT == 1, "scene_dsl's inlined curve flag bit positions (0, 1) drifted from engine.parallax.CURVE_FLAG_OP_BIT / CURVE_FLAG_ACTIVE_BIT — scene_band() packs both into band_curve.bc_flags and the runtime btsts them back out")
// P3 Task 12 adds one of the same species. scene()'s factor0_lock verification spells the
// locked factor as $0FF for the reason above; this holds that literal to parallax_dsl's
// own encoding (FACTOR_0 = FACTOR_LOCKED = $0FF, s1 15 = "term zero"). ONE ENCODING FOR
// BOTH PLANES: Decode_Factor_A and Decode_Factor_B are the same routine over different
// record bytes (parallax.emp), so the same $0FF is what "this plane cannot H-scroll"
// means on either of them — which is what lets the verification below scan `ly_fa`,
// `ly_fb` and a curve's far end against a single literal.
// FACTOR_0 resolves here without a `use`: parallax_dsl is a sigil COMPTIME_HELPERS member
// and is glob-injected into every module (parallax_dsl.emp:9-10).
ensure(FACTOR_0 == $0FF, "scene_dsl's inlined locked-factor encoding ($0FF) drifted from engine.level.parallax_dsl.FACTOR_0 — scene()'s factor0_lock verification compares every layer's ly_fa and ly_fb (and any curve's far end) against it, and a drift would let a scrolling plane claim the artifact cannot exist")

// LAYER_WORLD_Y_MAX — the engine-wide ceiling on an act's vertical extent, and the bound
// `layer()` replaced its `world_y < 512` with. It is NOT a parallax number and NOT a
// plane number: every act descriptor already asserts
// `(GRID_H << SECTION_SIZE_SHIFT) <= $8000` (games/.../act_descriptor.emp:86, cited by
// camera.emp:115 as the reason the camera's clamp arithmetic stays inside a signed word),
// so $8000 is the largest Y any act can declare and the largest the camera can reach.
// A top above it names a coordinate no camera state can produce.
//
// THIS IS THE LOOSE BOUND ON PURPOSE. The tight one is the act's OWN span, which layer()
// cannot see — it is handed to `assert_act_relative_tagged(scenes, act_span)` at the
// registry, where the act descriptor is in scope. Two guards, and the loose one is not
// decoration: it is what catches a `SceneLayer{ ly_world_y: … }` back-door value that
// never went through the registry fold.
pub const LAYER_WORLD_Y_MAX = $8000
// The other sits beside its own definition below and watches a RENUMBERING of the
// transition selectors this file both defines and inlines — a smaller risk, but a live
// one: an inserted selector is exactly the edit that would silently re-point scene()'s
// `transition` test at the wrong value.

// ---- capability bits (spec §3: the per-game cap mask the lowering folds against) ----
//
// THESE SIX DECLARATIONS ARE THE AUTHORITY, and P2 makes that literal rather than
// aspirational: tools/test_scene_span_labels.py and tools/effects_gates.py both PARSE
// the `pub const CAP_*` lines below and derive their expectations from what they find.
// Neither carries a copy of the mask, so adding a bit here (with a lowering and a
// bracketed block) needs no edit in either tool, and naming a span after a bit that is
// not declared here fails. The reserved block underneath is a COMMENT on purpose — a
// reserved bit has no lowering, so a span named for one would bracket a block that
// cannot exist, and the parse must not see it.
//
// WHY ONLY TWO OF THE SEVEN RESERVED BITS WERE PROMOTED (P3 Task 5). The promotion is
// what lets a span gate SEE a bit at all, so it has to precede the lowering that raises
// it — CAP_MULTI_DEFORM_TABLE is Task 7's per-layer deform refs and CAP_FACTOR_CURVE is
// Task 10's curves, and each of those tasks lands its gated block against a bit that is
// already parseable. The other five stayed in the comment for the symmetric reason:
// promoting a bit NOTHING raises manufactures a span gate with no subject, which is the
// vacuous-gate shape this suite keeps rediscovering. In the window between this parcel
// and Tasks 7/10 the two new bits are declared-but-ungated, and that state is REPORTED
// rather than hidden — effects_gates.py's scanline_spans lane emits an explicit
// "NOT GATED ANYWHERE" informational row per capability with no bracketed span, the same
// row CAP_TRANSITIONS carried before its parcel landed.
//
// P3 TASK 9 UPDATE — READ THIS BEFORE CONCLUDING ANYTHING FROM THAT ROW FOR
// CAP_MULTI_DEFORM_TABLE. The bit now HAS a lowering (`deform: Own(..)`) and a bracketed
// code site (`cap_multi_deform_table_band` in Parallax_Fill_PerLine), and the gate STILL
// reports "NOT GATED ANYWHERE" — correctly. The lane reads the two shipped `.lst`s, and
// neither game RAISES the bit: sonic4 stays $001F because adopting a per-layer table on a
// shipped scene is PARK-1 (owner-gated — it moves every ROM image in the tree), and demo
// stays 0. A span comptime-elided in both fixtures cannot appear in either listing, so
// for this bit the row is a statement about ADOPTION, not about the lowering. The
// bracket's own witness is the capability-raised instrument build recorded in
// docs/benchmarks/scanline-p3/DEFORM-OWN.md, where both labels appear in the listing.
//
// P3 TASK 10 UPDATE — AND THE ORIGINAL READING FOR CAP_FACTOR_CURVE IS NOW WRONG IN THE
// SAME WAY. This block used to say "if that bit is still showing the row after Task 10, the
// lowering did not happen". It landed, and the row still says NOT GATED ANYWHERE, for the
// reason above: the bit has a lowering (`curve: SceneCurve.To(..)`) and THREE bracketed code
// sites (`cap_factor_curve_hoist` and `cap_factor_curve_split` in Parallax_Step4_Fill,
// `cap_factor_curve_band` in Parallax_Fill_PerLine), and neither shipped game raises it —
// adopting a ramping factor on a shipped scene is owner-gated, and it moves every ROM image.
// The bracket's own witness is the capability-raised instrument build recorded in
// docs/benchmarks/scanline-p3/CURVES.md, where all three span pairs appear in the listing.
//
// THE GENERAL RULE, now that both P3 bits have hit it: this row is a statement about
// ADOPTION, never about whether a lowering exists. A bit with no lowering and a bit with a
// lowering nobody uses produce the SAME row, and the lane cannot tell them apart, because a
// comptime-elided span is absent from the listing either way.
//
// ---- THE BRACKETING-LABEL EMISSION CONVENTION (P2 §3.3 / §8.2) ----
//
// Every capability-gated block in the ENGINE carries a pair of boundary labels:
//
//     if (Game.SCANLINE_CAPS & CAP_ANCHORS) != 0 {
//     .cap_anchors_overlay_begin:
//             ... the elidable region ...
//     .cap_anchors_overlay_end:
//     }
//
// SPAN NAME = `cap_<capability>_<site>`, the capability half lowercased from the
// CAP_* name below, the site half a short discriminator. The site token is not
// decoration: two labels of the same name in one proc is a redefinition, and several
// capabilities are gated at more than one site. Resolution back to a capability is by
// LONGEST matching prefix (`per_col_vsram_emit` -> CAP_PER_COL_VSRAM), which the test
// proves unambiguous over the declared set rather than assuming it.
//
// WHY LABELS COME FIRST AND GATING SECOND. §8.2's path-level span gates measure a
// gated region by its boundary symbols, and the flat `.lst` drops `$`-mangled locals
// unless something names them — which is why raster_source_gate had to hand-roll a
// resolver. A gate added without brackets is a specialisation no gate can see; a
// bracket added without a gate reports a specialisation that never happens. Both
// directions are checked in tools/test_scene_span_labels.py.
//
// The convention is documented HERE, beside the bits, because this file is the one a
// reader reaches for when asking what a capability means — not in the engine modules
// that happen to carry today's spans.
// RETIRED: CAP_PER_LINE=$0001 (2026-08-26, d-29-corrected). The per-line HScroll fill,
// its 896-byte DMA and reg $0B = %11 are UNCONDITIONAL now — the per-cell path the bit
// used to select against was deleted — so there is no engine code for the bit to elide
// and no scene demand for it to express. Bit 0 stays a HOLE rather than being re-used:
// every hand-derived mask in the tree (poison fixtures, the registry pin, the game
// binding) was re-derived by dropping $0001, and a bit that changed meaning under those
// numbers would be the copied-expectation defect in its worst form. tools/scene_spans.py
// parses this RETIRED line so the gapless-run check still holds over declared ∪ retired.
pub const CAP_PER_COL_VSRAM  = $0002
pub const CAP_DEFORM         = $0004
pub const CAP_ANCHORS        = $0008
pub const CAP_TRANSITIONS    = $0010
// P3 (Task 5): declared ahead of their lowerings — see the promotion note above.
pub const CAP_MULTI_DEFORM_TABLE = $0020
pub const CAP_FACTOR_CURVE       = $0040
// Reserved (P4+): CAP_FG_SPRITE_STRIPS=$0080, CAP_BGANIM_BOUND=$0100,
// CAP_DENSE_TIER=$0200, CAP_COMPUTED=$0400, CAP_DEGRADE=$0800
//
// THE LEFT-COLUMN POLICY (P3 Task 12) RAISES NO BIT, AND THAT IS A RULING, NOT AN
// OVERSIGHT. Factor0Lock and Accept demand nothing of the runtime — one is a verified
// impossibility claim, the other a decision to ship — so a bit for them would gate no
// code. SpriteMask WILL need a bit (its strip emitter is engine code a non-per-column
// game must elide), but its emission has not landed and scene() refuses the variant
// until it does; per the promotion note above, the bit arrives WITH its gated block in
// the emission parcel, never ahead of it. CAP_FG_SPRITE_STRIPS ($0080) is NOT that bit —
// it is design §2's FG parallax strips, a different mechanism, and stays reserved.

// ---- attachment enums (spec §2) ----
// Comptime enums with payloads, per the RasterOp precedent: variants are the
// authoring vocabulary; every attachment defaults to its explicit None variant,
// never to a `Label = 0` sentinel (a Label compared to an int is silently
// unevaluable — the preset.emp lesson — so 0-sentinels can never be guarded).
// Payload slots are positional; the order is documented per variant.

// SceneDeform is authored in TWO POSITIONS and the variants are NOT interchangeable
// between them. Design §2 lists three spellings; this enum is all three, and which
// position accepts which variant is guarded in the constructors, never left to reading:
//
//   SCENE level — `scene(deform_fg:/deform_bg:)`, one attachment per PLANE.
//       None                     no H-deform on that plane
//       Shared(table, speed)     the scene's plane-shared table (the shipped twenty)
//       Own(..)                  REFUSED by scene() — it is a per-LAYER attachment
//
//   LAYER level — `layer(deform:)`, one attachment per layer (P3 Task 9).
//       None                     inherit the scene's plane tables. THIS IS DESIGN §2's
//                                `shared(phase)`: the layer already carries its own
//                                `phase` (ly_phase) and its own amplitude shifts
//                                (ly_dsa/ly_dsb), so "samples the scene's plane-shared
//                                table at a per-layer phase" needs no payload here and
//                                is what WindyHaze / SkyHaze / haze_fg already are. It
//                                does NOT trip CAP_MULTI_DEFORM_TABLE.
//       Own(table, sa, sb, ph, sp)  this layer samples ITS OWN table, at its own
//                                amplitude shifts, phase and speed. TRIPS
//                                CAP_MULTI_DEFORM_TABLE and rides Task 8's extended
//                                band record (band_ext's two table pointers + two
//                                speed bytes; the shifts and the phase land in the
//                                LEGACY prefix, which already has fields for them).
//       Shared(..)               REFUSED by layer() — a Label-carrying attachment at
//                                layer level is either `Own` (its own table) or `None`
//                                (the scene's); a third spelling would be a second way
//                                to say one of those two.
//
// ONE TABLE SERVES BOTH PLANES in both payload-carrying variants, which is why `Own`
// carries one Label and not two. That is `Shared`'s documented shape and the runtime's:
// a band's FG and BG curves are two pointers, and a layer that wants a different curve
// per plane is a §9 future, not a silent third meaning of this payload.
//
// NEVER A `Label = 0` DEFAULT. "Is a per-layer table attached?" is answered by
// `scene_deform_is_own()` below — a VARIANT test — because an `ensure` comparing a Label
// to an int is silently unevaluable and always passes (preset.emp:105-111,
// docs/EMP_PITFALLS.md §3). The payload is never the discriminator.
pub comptime enum SceneDeform {
    None,                       // no H-deform on this scene / inherit at layer level
    Shared(Label, int),         // (deform table Label, sample speed) — one table both planes
    Own(Label, int, int, int, int),  // (table, shift_a, shift_b, phase, speed) — PER-LAYER
}

pub comptime enum SceneVDeform {
    None,                       // no per-column V-deform
    Columns(Label, int, int),   // (column table Label, sample speed, amplitude shift)
}

pub comptime enum SceneAnchor {
    None,                       // no world-anchored band split
    At(int, int, int),          // (patch channel, anchored dsa, anchored dsb)
}

// SceneCurve — design §2's `curve(from, to)` on a BG scroll factor (P3 Task 10).
//
//   None      the BG factor holds for the whole layer. Every shipped layer.
//   To(fb2)   the effective Plane-B scroll RAMPS across the layer, from the layer's own
//             `fb` at its top to `fb2` at its bottom. Trips CAP_FACTOR_CURVE.
//
// WHY THE VARIANT CARRIES ONLY THE FAR END, when the design names the mechanism
// `curve(from, to)`. `from` IS the layer's `fb` argument — the packed factor already in
// the band record's legacy prefix, still the band's camera-tracked base scroll, still
// decoded by Decode_Factor_B exactly as it is for a flat layer. Design §2 is explicit that
// the base term is PRESERVED and the curve is an additive delta over it, so authoring
// `from` a second time inside the payload would be two sources for one record byte — the
// drift `layer()`'s own() guards were written to refuse (P3 Task 9). The spelling is
// therefore `layer(fb: <from>, curve: SceneCurve.To(<to>))`, one source per value.
//
// BG ONLY, and that is the design's word, not a simplification: "A BG factor may be a
// curve(from, to)". Plane A is hard-locked to the camera (`-Camera_X`, never lerped —
// parallax.emp's band loop says why: any FG offset drags the plane-wrap seam on screen),
// so a ramping FG factor is not a thing this engine can express at all.
//
// A LAYER MAY HAVE A CURVE OR A DEFORM REF, NOT BOTH (design §2), and `layer()` refuses the
// combination — see the guards there for the MEASURED register argument, which is not the
// one the design doc states.
//
// NEVER A `0` SENTINEL. "Is this layer a curve?" is `scene_curve_is_none()`, a VARIANT
// test, for the reason SceneDeform's banner gives: 0 is a legal packed factor field value
// and a payload can never be the discriminator.
pub comptime enum SceneCurve {
    None,
    To(int),                    // packed FACTOR_* the BG factor ramps TO at the layer bottom
}

// SceneVSplit — design §2's PER-LAYER VERTICAL DEPTH (P3 Task 11), and the only attachment
// in this model that lowers to a RASTER PROGRAM rather than to a band record.
//
//   None      Plane B's vertical scroll is whatever the VBlank writer put there. Every
//             shipped layer.
//   At(v)     from this layer's top DOWN, Plane B's whole-plane vertical scroll is `v`.
//             Lowers to ONE `fx_vscroll_split(line, v)` fire — the raster op that already
//             existed and was called by hand from level data (games/sonic4/data/effects/
//             ojz_effects.emp) with nothing lowering into it.
//
// WHAT IT IS NOT: a second runtime mechanism. `scene_vsplit_fires()` below returns FIRES,
// built by calling `fx_vscroll_split` itself — so the words, the spin solve, the landing
// check, the density guard and the axis-4b HInt total are the raster DSL's, unchanged and
// unduplicated. The acceptance proof for that claim is a byte-identity pin: the shipped
// hand-authored `OJZ_TestVsram` program is re-expressed through this attachment and the
// lowered words compared against the hand twin (games/sonic4/data/effects/ojz_effects.emp,
// `OJZ_VSRAM_VIA_SCENE`). A lowering that had grown its own encoder could not pass it.
//
// ---- THE TWO-WRITER RULING (P3 Task 11 Step 2). READ THIS BEFORE AUTHORING ONE. ----
//
// Plane B's vertical scroll word (VSRAM entry 1) has TWO writers in this engine and always
// has had: `Parallax_Step5_Vscroll` computes it in VBlank and `Vscroll_Write` ships it at
// frame top, while a vscroll-split fire writes it MID-FRAME from the HBlank handler. Before
// this attachment the collision was unauthorable — the split was only reachable by hand from
// a game's effects module, which knows nothing about the scene. Lowering makes it authorable,
// so the ruling ships here, and it is BOTH a refusal and a precedence:
//
//   REFUSED (scene(), two ensures):
//     * a scene whose Plane B TRACKS THE CAMERA (`v_factor != 15`). The VBlank writer's value
//       is a function of Camera_Y; the split's is a baked constant at a baked line. They do
//       not merely disagree — the split cannot express what the other writer is for.
//     * a scene attaching a per-column V-deform table. In per-column mode VSRAM entry 1 is
//       PLANE B OF COLUMN 0, not the plane, so a whole-plane write below the line would move
//       one 16-px column of forty.
//
//   PRECEDENCE, for what remains legal (a LOCKED plane, `v_factor: 15`): the VBlank writer
//   governs screen lines 0..N and the mid-frame write governs N+1..223, where N is the fire
//   line and the authored screen line is N+1 (the VSRAM landing model — a fire on line N
//   lands at N+1). That is renderable truth rather than a convention: the two writes happen
//   at different times in the same frame and the raster beam has already passed rows 0..N
//   when the second one lands, so each row is drawn under exactly one of them. And it does
//   not accumulate: `Vscroll_Write` re-asserts the base EVERY VBlank, so the split is a
//   per-frame transient delta over a value the other writer owns outright.
//
//   Both halves are asserted by an INSTRUMENT, not by this comment:
//   `tools/vsplit_landing_gate.py` captures rendered scanlines around the boundary and
//   checks (a) the first row the split governs is the authored line, (b) every row above it
//   is untouched by the split, and (c) neither drifts frame to frame.
//
// NO CAPABILITY BIT, and that is a ruling too. A CAP_* bit exists to ELIDE ENGINE CODE for a
// game that does not use a mechanism (the bracketing convention above). This lowering elides
// nothing: it emits a fire into a raster program the handler already interprets op-by-op,
// and there is no vscroll-split-shaped block in the engine to gate. A bit here would be a
// span gate with no span — the vacuous shape the promotion note above refuses.
//
// IT TOUCHES NEITHER THE HSCROLL BUFFER NOR THE BAND TOPS: a vertical split is a VSRAM
// write from a raster fire, and nothing about the (always per-line) HScroll fill changes
// for it.
//
// NEVER A `0` SENTINEL — `scene_vsplit_is_none()` is a VARIANT test, for the reason
// SceneDeform's and SceneCurve's banners give: 0 is a legal vertical scroll value (the top
// of the plane) and a payload can never be the discriminator.
pub comptime enum SceneVSplit {
    None,
    At(int),                    // whole-plane Plane-B vscroll in force from this top down
}

// SceneLeftColMask — design §2's MANDATORY left-column policy (P3 Task 12), and the one
// attachment in this model that is a DECLARATION about a hardware artifact rather than a
// mechanism request.
//
// READ THIS FIRST — THE ARTIFACT THIS FAMILY WAS BUILT AROUND IS FIXED (2026-08-29).
// `Parallax_Step5_Vscroll`'s column-19 borrow writes plane A's V-scroll into column-pair
// 19's plane-B word, so the leading sliver renders `camY & camY = camY` — the foreground's
// own scroll — on EVERY per-column scene, unconditionally, whatever the scene declares.
// The owner ruled for the repair over the sprite bar and over acceptance (d-40). So what a
// per-column scene declares here is NO LONGER "what do you do about the leftmost column";
// it is "you have seen what the repair costs elsewhere". §"What each arm means now", below.
//
// THE ARTIFACT: the VDP's per-column V-scroll grain is 16 px (one VSRAM entry pair per
// column-pair). When a plane carries HScroll that is not a multiple of 16 AND per-column
// V-scroll is on, that plane's leading sliver — `hscroll & 15` pixels, not a full column —
// renders at `VSRAM[$4C] & VSRAM[$4E]`, the bitwise AND of column-pair 19's two words, the
// same value for both planes, H40 only. Hardware-tested once, by Eke-Eke on a PAL MD2 in
// 2010; nobody has re-tested it on a Model 1 or a late revision in the sixteen years since,
// and the two claims that late hardware behaves differently (Plutiedev: "model 2 VA4
// onwards"; Stef: "genesis 3 VA3") disagree with each other and cite no test. The borrow is
// inert rather than wrong under every one of those readings. Full source tiering:
// docs/research/2026-08-29-vsram-column19-borrow.md. Commercial games either covered the
// sliver with a sprite strip (Battle Mania 2, Sonic 3's Hydrocity boss arena) or shipped it
// (Gynoug); no shipped game in this workspace's ten reference disassemblies uses the borrow.
// See engine/level/parallax.emp's Vscroll_Write banner for the mitigation list.
//
// WHAT THE REPAIR COSTS, because that is what an arm now adjudicates: column-pair 19 is
// also the entry that renders the screen's RIGHT edge, so plane B's rightmost 16 px now
// carry plane A's V-scroll. On this game's per-column scenes plane B is vertically LOCKED
// (v_factor 15, v_offset 0) while plane A tracks camY, so that is a displacement of
// camY mod 512 — a background seam, not a missing wobble. It is 16 px however it is
// arranged: a plane-B HScroll off the grain moves part of it to plane B's own left sliver.
//
// Design §2 makes the answer MANDATORY on any scene that attaches SceneVDeform.Columns:
// scene() refuses a per-column scene whose policy is Undeclared, and refuses a policy on a
// scene with no per-column V-deform (a policy for an artifact the scene cannot produce is
// noise that reads as coverage). The failing ensure elaborates at the scene's own
// constructor call, so the build error's source line names the scene.
//
// WHAT EACH ARM MEANS NOW (2026-08-29). The engine repairs the sliver on every per-column
// scene, so no arm can change what the leftmost column renders. What the arms still
// distinguish is whether the scene HAS a sliver at all, and whether its author has looked
// at the repair's right-edge price.
//
//   Undeclared    the default, and NEVER legal on a per-column scene. It exists because
//                 the default must not silently pick a policy — an enum whose default is
//                 one of the real answers is a declaration nobody made.
//   SpriteMask    cover the sliver with an opaque sprite strip, the way Battle Mania 2
//                 shipped it. THE OWNER RULED THIS OUT — d-40, 2026-08-29: he was offered
//                 the bar, the repair and acceptance, and rejected the bar in as many
//                 words. scene() still refuses the variant, and the refusal now cites the
//                 ruling rather than an emission parcel: the emission is not "pending", it
//                 is cancelled, and the borrow does what it was going to do. The arm
//                 survives this parcel only because the borrow's own right-edge price has
//                 not been looked at on a screen yet — see THE OPEN RETIREMENT below.
//                 What the never-built emission would have had to honour, kept because it
//                 is the expensive half of the knowledge and it is not written down twice:
//                 (1) OPAQUE sprites at screen X 0 (SAT X = 128, priority set, first in the
//                 link chain) — the VDP's X=0 sprite-MASKING feature suppresses later
//                 SPRITES on covered lines and cannot repaint a PLANE pixel, so it fails
//                 this job, and its first-sprite-on-line exemption fails it again;
//                 (2) full height is 7 SAT entries at an 8x32 mask geometry, not the 1 the
//                 design priced. Both are in docs/DEFERRED_WORK.md §"Sprite mask for
//                 per-column V-scroll" with the axis-5 reservation in
//                 tools/effects_budget_model.toml.
//   Factor0Lock   the artifact CANNOT OCCUR, and scene() VERIFIES the claim rather than
//                 filing it. THE CLAIM IS ABOUT BOTH PLANES, because the artifact is:
//                 per-column V-scroll is one register bit for the whole screen, but the
//                 partial column appears on a plane exactly when THAT plane's HScroll is
//                 not a multiple of the 16-px grain, and each plane's HScroll is its own
//                 sum (Decode_Factor_A/B plus that plane's deform sample). So the
//                 verification is four conditions, symmetric across the planes: every
//                 real layer's `fa` AND `fb` must be FACTOR_0, no layer may RAMP its
//                 plane-B factor to a non-FACTOR_0 far end (`curve:`), and no live
//                 H-deform amplitude may be reachable on EITHER plane — deform adds
//                 per-line HScroll ON TOP of the locked factor, so a locked base under a
//                 live `dsa`/`dsb` brings the sliver back on exactly those rows.
//                 A scene that cannot pass all four (Rocking's zero-table dsb,
//                 Perspective's shimmer rows, and BOTH families' `fa: FACTOR_1`
//                 foreground) spells Accept; the claim stays checkable or it is not
//                 available.
//
//                 EXPECT THIS TO BE HARD TO CLAIM, and read that as the answer rather
//                 than as a defect in the guard. A side-scrolling scene's foreground
//                 tracks the camera, so `fa` is FACTOR_1 and the lock is simply false
//                 there; a truthful Factor0Lock needs a scene whose FOREGROUND does not
//                 H-scroll either (a fixed arena, a locked cutscene backdrop). No scene
//                 in this tree is one, so Accept is the only arm a scrolling per-column
//                 scene can spell.
//
//                 SINCE THE BORROW IT CARRIES A WART, AND IT IS BOOKED RATHER THAN
//                 PAPERED OVER: the borrow is unconditional, so a Factor0Lock scene —
//                 which by construction has no sliver on either plane and needs no
//                 repair — still pays the right-edge seam. That costs nothing today
//                 (zero scenes declare it) and it is one of the two reasons the family
//                 is booked for retirement rather than kept.
//
//                 UNTIL 2026-08-28 THE VERIFICATION READ `fb`/`dsb` ONLY. A scene could
//                 lock plane B, declare Factor0Lock, pass the build, and still lose its
//                 leftmost 16 px on plane A — the guard certifying a claim about the
//                 plane nobody was looking at. Nothing shipped was mis-certified (both
//                 per-column families spell Accept); the hole was in what the build
//                 would have ADMITTED. Derivation:
//                 docs/research/2026-08-28-deform-scene-left-edge-triage.md.
//   Accept        ACCEPT THE RESIDUAL — and that is a REDEFINITION, 2026-08-29, not a
//                 rewording. It used to mean "ship the artifact", the Gynoug answer, and
//                 that meaning died with the borrow: the engine no longer ships the
//                 foreground artifact on any scene, so a scene declaring Accept does not
//                 get what the old word promised. What it now accepts is the borrow's
//                 PRICE — plane B's 16 px at the right edge, rendered at plane A's
//                 V-scroll (see the cost paragraph at the top). Both shipped per-column
//                 families spell it, and it is still a real decision, because that seam
//                 is a visible thing an author is agreeing to.
//
// THE OPEN RETIREMENT, so nobody re-derives it. With the borrow unconditional, NO arm here
// changes what the machine does — Factor0Lock and Accept differ only in what they assert
// about the scene, and both scenes get the same VSRAM. A mandatory declaration that cannot
// change behaviour is ceremony that reads as coverage, so the structurally right end state
// is to retire this family outright: the enum, the sc_left_col_mask field, the mandatory
// ensure, the five Factor0Lock guards, the two poison modules, the axis-5 SpriteMask rows,
// the six Accept spellings, and the schema key tools/effects_gen.py accepts from Aurora.
// THAT IS NOT DONE HERE ON PURPOSE. The borrow's right-edge price has never been seen on a
// screen; if the owner looks at it and rejects it, the borrow becomes per-config (a
// config-record flag, a byte-moving paired landing) and Accept comes straight back as
// "decline the borrow" with a mechanism behind it. Deleting an authoring surface and a
// cross-repo schema key the day before it might be needed is churn, not cleanliness.
// Booked in docs/DEFERRED_WORK.md; derivation in
// docs/research/2026-08-29-vsram-column19-borrow.md §5.
//
// NEVER A `0` SENTINEL — the questions are asked by the scene_lcm_* VARIANT tests below,
// for the reason every enum banner in this file gives (EMP_PITFALLS §3): the policy has no
// payload at all, so a sentinel would be indistinguishable from a declaration.
pub comptime enum SceneLeftColMask {
    Undeclared,
    SpriteMask,
    Factor0Lock,
    Accept,
}

// ---- lowering-tier and transition selectors ----
// `precision: cell | line` LIVED HERE until 2026-08-26 (PRECISION_CELL / PRECISION_LINE and
// the Scene.sc_precision field). It was retired with the per-cell HScroll path under owner
// ruling d-29-corrected: the field's only consumer was arm 4 of the per-line forcer, and
// with one fill there is nothing to force. Aurora's scene schema still spells the field;
// tools/effects_gen.py accepts and IGNORES it, and its removal from the schema is booked
// as Aurora's in docs/DEFERRED_WORK.md.
pub const TRANS_SMOOTH   = 0    // lerp the section transition
pub const TRANS_INSTANT  = 1    // snap on the promote frame
// These two are DEFINED here, but scene()'s default and the test that reads it are
// spelled `0` / `1` so a caller's import style cannot change what a defaulted argument
// means. Same pin discipline as the imported-name block above.
ensure(TRANS_SMOOTH == 0 && TRANS_INSTANT == 1, "scene_dsl's inlined transition selectors drifted from TRANS_SMOOTH / TRANS_INSTANT")

// -----------------------------------------------
// SceneLayer — one authored world-Y layer (comptime-only; never emitted:
// the Task-3 lowering folds SceneLayers into parallax_config/band_entry).
//
// no hand-building: use layer() or no_layer() (the span ensure lives there).
// This type has to be `pub` — it is the element type in scene()'s signature — so the
// back door is open by construction: `SceneLayer{ ly_world_y: 5, .. }` type-checks and
// skips layer()'s range guard entirely. Same convention as raster_dsl.emp:90, and for the
// same reason: the guards are in the constructor.
//
// SINCE P3 TASK 7 THE BACK DOOR IS NARROWER, and worth stating because the old note here
// claimed something that is no longer the failure mode. It used to say a hand-built value
// "truncates to a band top 5 px off, because the `>> 3` in scene_band() is exact only for
// a world_y that layer() already checked" — there is no `>> 3` any more, and an off-grid
// top is legal. What a back-door value now escapes is only the RANGE check, and
// `scene_plane_line()` catches an unmappable one at the lowering AND at the registry fold
// (assert_act_relative_tagged calls it). A value that is in range is simply a legal top.
// -----------------------------------------------
pub struct SceneLayer {
    ly_world_y:  u16,   // authored layer top, ACT world pixels (was plane pixels pre-P3-T7)
    ly_fa:       u16,   // packed FACTOR_* (9 bits), plane A
    ly_fb:       u16,   // packed FACTOR_* (9 bits), plane B
    ly_dsa:      u8,    // deform-amplitude shift, plane A (15 = none)
    ly_dsb:      u8,    // deform-amplitude shift, plane B (15 = none)
    ly_phase:    u8,    // deform sample-index desync
    ly_enabled:  u8,    // 0 = dormant layer
    // P3 TASK 9 — the per-layer deform ref. `None` = inherit the scene's plane tables,
    // which IS design §2's `shared(phase)` (the phase is ly_phase, one field up).
    // `Own(..)` gives this layer its own table+speed and is the ONLY thing in the model
    // that raises CAP_MULTI_DEFORM_TABLE. Comptime-only like the rest of this struct:
    // the table and speed lower into band_ext, the shifts and phase into the legacy
    // prefix fields above.
    ly_deform:   SceneDeform,
    // P3 TASK 10 — the BG scroll factor's optional ramp. `None` = `ly_fb` holds for the
    // whole layer (every shipped layer); `To(fb2)` ramps from `ly_fb` to `fb2` across the
    // layer and is the ONLY thing that raises CAP_FACTOR_CURVE. Comptime-only like the
    // rest of this struct: it lowers into band_curve's two factor bytes and its flags.
    ly_curve:    SceneCurve,
    // P3 TASK 11 — the per-layer VERTICAL depth. `None` = Plane B's vertical scroll is
    // whatever the VBlank writer put there (every shipped layer); `At(v)` snaps it to `v`
    // from this layer's top down. It is the ONE field in this struct that lowers to a
    // RASTER PROGRAM rather than to a band record — `scene_vsplit_fires()` turns it into an
    // `fx_vscroll_split` fire — so it reaches no `band_entry` field at all and cannot move
    // a config byte. See the SceneVSplit banner for the two-writer ruling.
    ly_vsplit:   SceneVSplit,
}

// -----------------------------------------------
// layer — one scene layer at an authored world-Y top.
// world_y: layer top in ACT world pixels. Free of the 8-px grid since P3 Task 7.
// fa/fb:   packed FACTOR_* scroll factors for planes A/B (parallax_dsl)
// dsa/dsb: per-plane deform-amplitude shifts (15 = no deform on that plane)
// phase:   deform sample-index desync; enabled: 0 authors a dormant layer
//
// ---- P3 TASK 7 RELAXED BOTH GUARDS. NEITHER WAS DELETED. ----
//
// (1) `world_y % 8 == 0` IS GONE, AND ITS JOB MOVED RATHER THAN VANISHING. The guard
//     existed because P1's `band_top_cell` was a Plane-B CELL ROW, so an off-grid top
//     silently truncated. The record now carries a PLANE LINE (parallax.emp's
//     `band_top_plane`, u16) and Step 4a rebases in lines, so an off-cell top is
//     REPRESENTABLE, and the per-line fill places it on the exact line. (Between P3
//     Task 7 and 2026-08-26 an off-grid top also FORCED the per-line pipeline through
//     `scene_forces_per_line()` arm 5, because the then-extant per-cell fill could only
//     place a cell edge; the per-cell fill and the forcer are deleted, d-29-corrected.)
//
// (2) `world_y < 512` BECAME A DERIVED CEILING, and the 512 was the wrong space anyway:
//     it was the BG PLANE's span, and world_y is an ACT coordinate. The bound here is
//     the engine-wide act-axis ceiling (LAYER_WORLD_Y_MAX, below); the TIGHT per-act
//     bound cannot live in this fn — layer() sees one layer and knows nothing about the
//     act — so it is enforced by `assert_act_relative_tagged(scenes, act_span)` at the
//     registry, where the act descriptor's own `GRID_H << SECTION_SIZE_SHIFT` reaches it.
//     Two bounds, both derived, neither typed out of the air.
// -----------------------------------------------
pub comptime fn layer(world_y: int, fa: int, fb: int, dsa: int = 15, dsb: int = 15,
                      phase: int = 0, enabled: int = 1,
                      deform: SceneDeform = SceneDeform.None,
                      curve: SceneCurve = SceneCurve.None,
                      vsplit: SceneVSplit = SceneVSplit.None) -> SceneLayer {
    // $8000 is inlined per the pin block at the top of this file (LAYER_WORLD_Y_MAX).
    ensure(world_y >= 0 && world_y < $8000,
           "layer(): world_y {world_y} is outside the engine's act-axis span (0 .. $8000). Every act descriptor asserts (GRID_H << SECTION_SIZE_SHIFT) <= $8000 and the camera's world coordinates are signed words, so a top above it names a Y the camera can never reach. NOTE this is the ENGINE-WIDE ceiling: the tight bound is this game's own act span and it is checked at the registry by assert_act_relative_tagged()")
    // ---- P3 TASK 9: the per-layer deform ref, and its three guards ----
    let is_own = scene_deform_is_own(deform)
    // (1) POSITION. `Shared` is the SCENE-level spelling and means "the scene's
    //     plane-shared table"; at layer level that is what `None` already means, so a
    //     Shared here would be a second spelling of one of the two legal answers — and,
    //     worse, one carrying a Label the lowering would have to pick a plane for.
    //     Refused by VARIANT, never by payload.
    ensure(is_own == 1 || scene_deform_is_none(deform) == 1,
           "layer(): deform: Shared(..) is the SCENE-level attachment — pass it to scene(deform_fg:/deform_bg:). At layer level a shared table is spelled `deform: SceneDeform.None` (the default): the layer already carries its own phase and amplitude shifts, which is exactly design §2's shared(phase). Use SceneDeform.Own(table, shift_a, shift_b, phase, speed) to give this layer its OWN table")
    // (2) ONE SOURCE FOR THREE FIELDS. own()'s shift_a/shift_b/phase lower into the SAME
    //     record fields as this fn's dsa/dsb/phase arguments (band_deform_shift_a/b,
    //     band_phase_offset). Authoring both is two sources for one byte, so the plain
    //     arguments must be left at their defaults when own() supplies them. This is the
    //     drift the tree keeps paying for spelled as a build error.
    ensure(is_own == 0 || (dsa == 15 && dsb == 15 && phase == 0),
           "layer(): deform: Own(..) already carries shift_a, shift_b and phase, and they lower into the SAME record fields as this call's dsa ({dsa}) / dsb ({dsb}) / phase ({phase}) arguments. Leave those three at their defaults (15, 15, 0) and author the values inside own(), or drop own() and author them here — two sources for one byte is how they drift")
    let own_sa = scene_deform_own_shift_a(deform)
    let own_sb = scene_deform_own_shift_b(deform)
    // (3) AN OWN TABLE NOTHING SAMPLES IS NOT AN ATTACHMENT, IT IS A MISTAKE — and this
    //     guard is also what makes CAP_MULTI_DEFORM_TABLE ⇒ CAP_DEFORM structural rather
    //     than hopeful. The scene-level flat-pathed idiom (a zero table at shift 15)
    //     used to exist purely to force the per-line pipeline; since 2026-08-26 the
    //     pipeline IS per-line for every scene, so a table nothing samples buys nothing
    //     at either level.
    //     A per-layer table exists to be sampled BY THAT LAYER; with both shifts at 15 it
    //     costs a record extension and an inner-loop reload for a curve nothing reads.
    //     scene_caps() pins the implication that follows from this.
    ensure(is_own == 0 || own_sa != 15 || own_sb != 15,
           "layer(): deform: Own(..) attaches a table this layer never samples — both amplitude shifts are 15 (the no-deform sentinel). A per-layer table costs a band_ext extension in every band of the game AND a per-band pointer reload in the fill loop, so it must be sampled by the layer that owns it. (The fill is per-line for every scene since 2026-08-26, so there is nothing a flat table could be forcing)")
    ensure(is_own == 0 || (own_sa >= 0 && own_sa <= 15 && own_sb >= 0 && own_sb <= 15),
           "layer(): deform: Own(..) shift_a {own_sa} / shift_b {own_sb} outside 0..15 — the runtime samples with asr.w, so a wider shift saturates to the sign bit: a wrong picture, not a fault")
    let own_ph = scene_deform_own_phase(deform)
    ensure(is_own == 0 || (own_ph >= 0 && own_ph <= 255),
           "layer(): deform: Own(..) phase {own_ph} outside 0..255 — band_phase_offset is a u8 and the sample index is mod-256")
    let own_sp = scene_deform_speed(deform)
    ensure(is_own == 0 || (own_sp >= 0 && own_sp <= 255),
           "layer(): deform: Own(..) speed {own_sp} outside 0..255 — bx_deform_speed_a/b are u8")
    // THE FOLD. Single-level if-expressions, one per field, never a nested one (an `if`
    // in block-tail position yields unit SILENTLY — docs/EMP_PITFALLS.md §1). After this
    // point an own() layer is indistinguishable from a hand-authored one to every
    // amplitude scan in this file, which is the property that keeps the folds unchanged.
    let eff_dsa   = if is_own == 1 { own_sa } else { dsa }
    let eff_dsb   = if is_own == 1 { own_sb } else { dsb }
    let eff_phase = if is_own == 1 { own_ph } else { phase }
    // ---- P3 TASK 10: the curve, and its four guards ----
    //
    // (1) CURVE ∧ DEFORM IS REFUSED PER LAYER (design §2), AND THE DESIGN'S STATED REASON
    //     IS NOT THE MEASURED ONE. §2 says "the fill loop's register file is exhausted
    //     (verified: .lp_both uses all 16)". `.lp_both` uses FOURTEEN — d0-d7 and a1-a6.
    //     a0 is spilled at the proc's entry `movem.l a0/d7,-(sp)` and is dormant in that
    //     loop (the two single-channel loops DO use it, as their curve walk pointer since
    //     the unroll parcel); a7 only brackets the proc. So "all 16" is checkably false and
    //     a guard resting on it would be deleted by the next reader.
    //
    //     THE REAL POSITION IS THE CURVE LOOP'S OWN ALLOCATION, measured against
    //     `.lp_curve` as built (parallax.emp): it spends SEVEN data registers — the
    //     accumulator, the whole step, the Bresenham remainder and error, the span
    //     modulus, the constant FG word and the line index — plus a4 (output cursor) and
    //     a0 (output end), with d7 carrying the band countdown. That is every data
    //     register. Sampling either channel additionally needs a phase base, an amplitude
    //     shift and a sample scratch: three more against zero free. The combination is a
    //     §9 future needing a measured re-allocation, exactly as the design says — for a
    //     reason a reader can check.
    let is_curve = 1 - scene_curve_is_none(curve)
    ensure(is_curve == 0 || (eff_dsa == 15 && eff_dsb == 15),
           "layer(): this layer authors BOTH a curve and a live deform amplitude (dsa {eff_dsa} / dsb {eff_dsb}; 15 is the no-deform sentinel). Design §2 forbids curve AND deform on one layer: the fill's curve loop already spends all seven usable data registers (accumulator, whole step, Bresenham remainder and error, span modulus, constant FG word, line index) plus two address registers, and a sampled channel needs three more. Give the deform to a different layer, or drop the curve")
    // (2) A curve layer may not carry a per-layer deform TABLE either. Implied by (1) —
    //     layer() already refuses an own() whose two shifts are both 15 — but spelled
    //     separately so the diagnostic names the attachment the author actually wrote
    //     rather than the amplitude it folded into.
    ensure(is_curve == 0 || is_own == 0,
           "layer(): this layer authors both `curve:` and `deform: Own(..)`. Design §2 forbids curve ∧ deform on one layer (see the register argument at the guard above), and an own() table that nothing samples is refused in its own right")
    // (3) THE FAR-END FACTOR IS A PACKED FACTOR_*, RANGE-CHECKED AS ONE. It lowers to
    //     band_curve's two shift bytes plus an op bit, and Decode_Factor_Curve reads them
    //     with the same `cmpi.b #15` sentinels Decode_Factor_A/B use — so a value outside
    //     the 9-bit encoding does not fail, it silently decodes as some other factor.
    let curve_to = scene_curve_to(curve)
    ensure(is_curve == 0 || (curve_to >= 0 && curve_to <= $1FF),
           "layer(): curve: To({curve_to}) is not a packed FACTOR_* — the encoding is 9 bits (bits 0-3 shift1, 4-7 shift2, bit 8 op) and a wider value silently truncates into a different factor. Use the FACTOR_* names from engine/level/parallax_dsl.emp")
    // (4) A CURVE THAT RAMPS NOWHERE IS NOT A CURVE, IT IS A MISTAKE — the same shape as
    //     own()'s both-shifts-15 guard, and for the same reason: it costs a band_curve tail
    //     in every band of the game, a per-frame divide, and a per-line Bresenham loop, to
    //     emit the identical words `.lp_flat` would have broadcast.
    ensure(is_curve == 0 || curve_to != fb,
           "layer(): curve: To({curve_to}) is the same factor as this layer's fb, so the ramp's two ends are equal and the emitted HScroll is byte-identical to the flat path. A curve costs a band_curve tail in EVERY band of the game plus a per-frame divide and a per-line accumulate; drop the curve, or ramp to a different factor")
    // (5) A DORMANT LAYER'S BAND INHERITS THE PREVIOUS BAND'S SCROLL WORDS (the walker's
    //     `.band_disabled` arm), so its base is not its own factor and a ramp from it means
    //     nothing — while the runtime would still take the curve loop, because the tail's
    //     active bit is authored, not derived from the mask.
    ensure(is_curve == 0 || enabled != 0,
           "layer(): a dormant layer (enabled: 0) may not carry a curve — a disabled band inherits the PREVIOUS band's scroll words instead of decoding its own factor, so there is no base for the ramp to be a delta over, yet the fill would still run the curve loop for it")
    // ---- P3 TASK 11: the one LAYER-level guard on the vertical split ----
    //
    // THE VALUE IS A PLANE-B ROW, AND THE PLANE WRAPS AT 512. VSRAM is read modulo the
    // plane's height, so `At(576)` is not a bigger scroll than `At(64)` — it IS `At(64)`,
    // silently, and the author who wrote it was reasoning about a taller plane than the one
    // that exists. Refusing the wider value is refusing a second spelling of a legal one.
    // 512 is inlined per the pin block at the top of this file (PLANE_B_SPAN).
    //
    // NEGATIVE IS REFUSED FOR THE SAME REASON RATHER THAN A DIFFERENT ONE: `At(-8)` reaches
    // the VDP as $FFF8 and is read as row 504 of the plane, which is expressible as At(504).
    let split_off = scene_vsplit_offset(vsplit)
    ensure(scene_vsplit_is_none(vsplit) == 1 || (split_off >= 0 && split_off < 512),
           "layer(): vsplit: At({split_off}) is outside the Plane-B span (0 .. 511). The VDP reads a vertical scroll value modulo the plane's height, so a wider or negative value is not a bigger scroll — it is silently one of the 512 rows that already had a spelling. Author the row you mean")
    return SceneLayer{ ly_world_y: world_y, ly_fa: fa, ly_fb: fb, ly_dsa: eff_dsa,
                       ly_dsb: eff_dsb, ly_phase: eff_phase, ly_enabled: enabled,
                       ly_deform: deform, ly_curve: curve, ly_vsplit: vsplit }
}

// no_layer — the padding element. `Scene.sc_layers` is a FIXED [SceneLayer; 16] (a
// struct field needs a concrete type; `array` is a parameter type only), so an author
// with four real layers still spells sixteen elements. This is the fifth through
// sixteenth:
// dormant, factor-locked, at world-Y 0. It is never lowered — scene_band() only ever
// indexes 0..sc_count — so its field values are inert with ONE exception: `ly_fa == 0`
// is load-bearing, and it is what scene()'s pad guard discriminates on. Every FACTOR_*
// is nonzero (parallax_dsl.emp:25-40; even FACTOR_LOCKED is $0FF), so a zero fa cannot
// come from any authored layer. Do not "tidy" it to FACTOR_LOCKED.
pub comptime fn no_layer() -> SceneLayer {
    return SceneLayer{ ly_world_y: 0, ly_fa: 0, ly_fb: 0, ly_dsa: 15,
                       ly_dsb: 15, ly_phase: 0, ly_enabled: 0,
                       ly_deform: SceneDeform.None, ly_curve: SceneCurve.None,
                       ly_vsplit: SceneVSplit.None }
}

// derived_mask — the layer mask the model implies: bit i per enabled layer below count.
//
// SHARED BY scene() AND scene_hdr() ON PURPOSE, not for brevity. scene() checks the
// sc_mask_raw bridge against it and scene_hdr() emits it; two copies of the loop could
// drift into a guard that approves a mask the lowering then does not produce, which is
// the one failure this pair of call sites exists to make impossible.
//
// Takes the ARRAY, not a Scene: scene() has no Scene yet when it needs this.
pub comptime fn derived_mask(layers: [SceneLayer; 16], count: int) -> int {
    comptime var mask = 0
    for i in 0..count {
        let l = layers[i]
        if l.ly_enabled != 0 {
            mask = mask | (1 << i)
        }
    }
    return mask
}

// derived_own — does ANY real layer carry its own deform table? (P3 Task 9.)
//
// SHARED BY scene() AND scene_caps() FOR derived_mask()'s REASON, not for brevity:
// scene() guards on the answer and the capability fold raises CAP_MULTI_DEFORM_TABLE on
// it, and two copies of this walk could drift into a scene the guard approves and the
// fold does not classify — which is exactly a wrong record shape with no diagnostic.
// Takes the ARRAY, because scene() has no Scene yet when it needs this.
//
// STOPS AT `count`, like every other fold here: the pad slots past it are no_layer()s
// (scene()'s pad guard proves it) and are lowered by nothing.
pub comptime fn derived_own(layers: [SceneLayer; 16], count: int) -> int {
    comptime var any = 0
    // The one-dot rule — field, then index, then field (see scene_caps()).
    for i in 0..count {
        let l = layers[i]
        if scene_deform_is_own(l.ly_deform) == 1 {
            any = 1
        }
    }
    return any
}

// derived_curve — does ANY real layer ramp its BG factor? (P3 Task 10.)
//
// SHARED BY scene() AND scene_caps() FOR derived_own()'s REASON: two copies of this walk
// could drift into a scene the guard approves and the fold classifies another way — a
// config whose guard and record shape disagree, which is the desync one derivation exists to make
// impossible. Takes the ARRAY, because scene() has no Scene yet when it needs this.
//
// STOPS AT `count`: the pad slots past it are no_layer()s, whose ly_curve is None anyway.
pub comptime fn derived_curve(layers: [SceneLayer; 16], count: int) -> int {
    comptime var any = 0
    // The one-dot rule — field, then index, then field (see scene_caps()).
    for i in 0..count {
        let l = layers[i]
        if scene_curve_is_none(l.ly_curve) == 0 {
            any = 1
        }
    }
    return any
}

// derived_vsplit — does ANY real layer carry a vertical split? (P3 Task 11.)
//
// SHARED BY scene() AND scene_vsplit_fires() FOR derived_own()'s REASON: scene() refuses the
// two-writer collisions on the answer and the fold builds the fires on it, and two copies of
// this walk could drift into a scene the guard approved and the fold lowered differently —
// here that would mean a fire the two-writer ruling never adjudicated. Takes the ARRAY,
// because scene() has no Scene yet when it needs this.
//
// STOPS AT `count`: the pad slots past it are no_layer()s, whose ly_vsplit is None anyway.
pub comptime fn derived_vsplit(layers: [SceneLayer; 16], count: int) -> int {
    comptime var any = 0
    // The one-dot rule — field, then index, then field (see scene_caps()).
    for i in 0..count {
        let l = layers[i]
        if scene_vsplit_is_none(l.ly_vsplit) == 0 {
            any = 1
        }
    }
    return any
}

// scene_vsplit_count — HOW MANY layers carry `vsplit: At(..)`, where `derived_vsplit`
// answers only WHETHER any does. Added 2026-08-27 because a consumer wanted to assert
// "the fold returned one fire per authoring layer" and had no way to say it: the only
// available helper was a 0/1 presence flag, so the assertion was written as a LITERAL
// count while its comment claimed to be derived. A scene that gains a vsplit is an
// authoring act; a scene whose fold returns FEWER fires than its layers author is a lost
// attachment. Only the second is a defect, and only a real count can tell them apart.
pub comptime fn scene_vsplit_count(s: Scene) -> int {
    // The one-dot rule — field, then index, then field (see scene_caps()).
    let ls = s.sc_layers
    let n  = s.sc_count
    comptime var seen = 0
    for i in 0..n {
        let l = ls[i]
        if scene_vsplit_is_none(l.ly_vsplit) == 0 {
            seen = seen + 1
        }
    }
    return seen
}

// =====================================================================
// Attachment accessors — the ONLY way a body reads an enum payload.
//
// `match` in .emp is EXPRESSION-form (ast.rs Expr::Match; there is no statement
// match), so a guard cannot be written inside an arm the way the spec sketch drew it.
// These accessors are the shape that survives: each destructures exhaustively into a
// plain int/Label, and scene() then does its `ensure`s at the top level over INTS.
//
// That is not merely a workaround, it is the correct spelling for the guards: an
// `ensure` comparing a `Label` to an int is silently unevaluable and always passes
// (preset.emp:105-111; configs.emp's hdr() says the same at its own guards). Every
// guard below therefore reads exactly like hdr()'s — `anchor_ch == $FF || ...` — which
// is also what keeps Task 7's hdr()/scene_hdr() equivalence proof honest: the two are
// comparing the same integers, not two different encodings of "no anchor".
//
// Each accessor's None arm returns hdr()'s OWN DEFAULT for that field, so the lowering
// is a straight copy and the byte-identity contract lives in one place per field.
// =====================================================================

// deform table Label; hdr() default 0 (= no table).
pub comptime fn scene_deform_table(d: SceneDeform) -> Label {
    return match d {
        None                          => 0,
        Shared(tbl, speed)            => tbl,
        Own(tbl, sa, sb, ph, sp)      => tbl,
    }
}
// deform sample speed; hdr() default ONE, not zero — a speed of 0 is a static table,
// which is a different (and shipped: v_deform_speed_bg) meaning.
pub comptime fn scene_deform_speed(d: SceneDeform) -> int {
    return match d {
        None                          => 1,
        Shared(tbl, speed)            => speed,
        Own(tbl, sa, sb, ph, sp)      => sp,
    }
}
// 1 when no table is attached. Exists so the capability fold and the cost model can
// test "is there a table?" without ever touching the Label.
pub comptime fn scene_deform_is_none(d: SceneDeform) -> int {
    return match d {
        None                          => 1,
        Shared(tbl, speed)            => 0,
        Own(tbl, sa, sb, ph, sp)      => 0,
    }
}
// THE PER-LAYER QUESTION, and the only correct way to ask it (P3 Task 9): is a table
// attached to THIS LAYER, as opposed to inherited from the scene? A VARIANT test — a
// `Label`-vs-int `ensure` is silently unevaluable and always passes, so the payload can
// never be the discriminator. Every consumer of the CAP_MULTI_DEFORM_TABLE bit, in the
// fold and in the constructors, goes through this.
pub comptime fn scene_deform_is_own(d: SceneDeform) -> int {
    return match d {
        None                          => 0,
        Shared(tbl, speed)            => 0,
        Own(tbl, sa, sb, ph, sp)      => 1,
    }
}
// Own()'s three LEGACY-PREFIX payloads. They are not new record fields: shift_a/shift_b
// and phase already exist as band_deform_shift_a/b and band_phase_offset, which is why
// `own(table, shift_a/b, phase, speed)` adds only the table and the speed to the extended
// record (docs/benchmarks/scanline-p3/EXTENDED-RECORD.md §6). `layer()` folds these into
// ly_dsa / ly_dsb / ly_phase so that EVERY existing scan — scene_caps()'s live_amp,
// scene_live_amp_a/b, scene_band()'s lowering — sees an own() layer's amplitude without
// knowing the variant exists. The None/Shared arms return layer()'s own defaults, so the
// fold is a straight substitution.
pub comptime fn scene_deform_own_shift_a(d: SceneDeform) -> int {
    return match d {
        None                          => 15,
        Shared(tbl, speed)            => 15,
        Own(tbl, sa, sb, ph, sp)      => sa,
    }
}
pub comptime fn scene_deform_own_shift_b(d: SceneDeform) -> int {
    return match d {
        None                          => 15,
        Shared(tbl, speed)            => 15,
        Own(tbl, sa, sb, ph, sp)      => sb,
    }
}
pub comptime fn scene_deform_own_phase(d: SceneDeform) -> int {
    return match d {
        None                          => 0,
        Shared(tbl, speed)            => 0,
        Own(tbl, sa, sb, ph, sp)      => ph,
    }
}

// THE CURVE QUESTION, asked of the VARIANT (P3 Task 10). 1 when the layer's BG factor
// holds for the whole layer — i.e. when the band lowers with the curve tail inert.
pub comptime fn scene_curve_is_none(c: SceneCurve) -> int {
    return match c {
        None      => 1,
        To(fb2)   => 0,
    }
}
// The far-end packed factor. The None arm returns $0FF = FACTOR_LOCKED (s1 15, s2 15,
// op 0), which is the packed encoding of "no scroll at all" — an inert value for the two
// ROM bytes of a band whose curve-active bit is clear and which the runtime never reads.
// Returning 0 instead would put s1 = 0 (= camX, the FASTEST factor) in a field a future
// reader might trust, which is the worse inert value to pick.
pub comptime fn scene_curve_to(c: SceneCurve) -> int {
    return match c {
        None      => $0FF,
        To(fb2)   => fb2,
    }
}

// THE VERTICAL-SPLIT QUESTION, asked of the VARIANT (P3 Task 11). 1 when this layer's top is
// not a vertical boundary — i.e. when the scene lowers no fire for it.
pub comptime fn scene_vsplit_is_none(v: SceneVSplit) -> int {
    return match v {
        None    => 1,
        At(off) => 0,
    }
}
// The Plane-B vertical scroll in force below the layer top. The None arm returns 0, and
// UNLIKE scene_curve_to()'s inert $0FF this default is never emitted anywhere: a None layer
// lowers to no fire at all, so there is no record for it to land in. It exists so `layer()`'s
// range guard can be written over an int without a second variant test in front of it.
pub comptime fn scene_vsplit_offset(v: SceneVSplit) -> int {
    return match v {
        None    => 0,
        At(off) => off,
    }
}

// THE LEFT-COLUMN QUESTIONS, asked of the VARIANT (P3 Task 12). Three tests, not one
// int-coded answer, because every consumer asks a yes/no question and an integer encoding
// would be a sentinel by another name. All four arms are spelled in each so a fifth
// variant fails every match here instead of silently defaulting.
pub comptime fn scene_lcm_is_undeclared(m: SceneLeftColMask) -> int {
    return match m {
        Undeclared  => 1,
        SpriteMask  => 0,
        Factor0Lock => 0,
        Accept      => 0,
    }
}
pub comptime fn scene_lcm_is_sprite_mask(m: SceneLeftColMask) -> int {
    return match m {
        Undeclared  => 0,
        SpriteMask  => 1,
        Factor0Lock => 0,
        Accept      => 0,
    }
}
pub comptime fn scene_lcm_is_factor0_lock(m: SceneLeftColMask) -> int {
    return match m {
        Undeclared  => 0,
        SpriteMask  => 0,
        Factor0Lock => 1,
        Accept      => 0,
    }
}

// V-column table Label; hdr() default 0 (= whole-plane V-scroll).
pub comptime fn scene_vdeform_table(v: SceneVDeform) -> Label {
    return match v {
        None                       => 0,
        Columns(tbl, speed, shift) => tbl,
    }
}
// V-column sample speed; hdr() default ZERO (static column shape). Note this is the
// opposite default from the H-deform speed above — both are reproduced literally.
pub comptime fn scene_vdeform_speed(v: SceneVDeform) -> int {
    return match v {
        None                       => 0,
        Columns(tbl, speed, shift) => speed,
    }
}
// V-column amplitude shift; hdr() default 4.
pub comptime fn scene_vdeform_shift(v: SceneVDeform) -> int {
    return match v {
        None                       => 4,
        Columns(tbl, speed, shift) => shift,
    }
}
pub comptime fn scene_vdeform_is_none(v: SceneVDeform) -> int {
    return match v {
        None                       => 1,
        Columns(tbl, speed, shift) => 0,
    }
}

// Anchor patch channel; hdr() default PARALLAX_ANCHOR_NONE = $FF (inlined per the pin
// block at the top of this file). THE INT THE GUARDS COMPARE.
pub comptime fn scene_anchor_ch(a: SceneAnchor) -> int {
    return match a {
        None              => $FF,
        At(ch, dsa, dsb)  => ch,
    }
}
// Deform shifts the overlay writes into every band at/below the anchored line;
// hdr() default 15 = no deform on that plane.
pub comptime fn scene_anchor_dsa(a: SceneAnchor) -> int {
    return match a {
        None              => 15,
        At(ch, dsa, dsb)  => dsa,
    }
}
pub comptime fn scene_anchor_dsb(a: SceneAnchor) -> int {
    return match a {
        None              => 15,
        At(ch, dsa, dsb)  => dsb,
    }
}

// -----------------------------------------------
// Scene — one authored world-Y scene (comptime-only, never emitted).
//
// TWO FIELDS ARE BYTE-IDENTITY BRIDGES, not model. They exist because shipped configs
// carry header values the authored model cannot derive, and P1's whole contract is that
// the ROM does not move a byte:
//
//   sc_mask_raw            ParallaxConfig_OJZ_Default AND ParallaxConfig_OJZ_Underwater
//                          (configs.emp:107, :132) both declare band_count 4 with
//                          layer_mask $1F — FIVE bits for FOUR bands. A mask derived
//                          from ly_enabled can only ever produce $0F, so Task 6 spells
//                          this bridge TWICE, once per config. -1 = derive.
//   sc_v_deform_shift_raw  ParallaxConfig_SkyHaze (configs.emp:269) passes
//                          v_deform_shift_bg: 0 while attaching NO v-deform table;
//                          SceneVDeform.None lowers to hdr()'s default 4, which is a
//                          different byte. -1 = derive. ONE config, one bridge.
//
// THE NEAR MISS, because it will be mistaken for a third: ParallaxConfig_OJZ_LockedClouds
// (configs.emp:299) declares band_count 5 with layer_mask $1E, and that is NOT a bridge —
// it DERIVES, from `enabled: 0` on layer 0, which is precisely the authored model working.
// Spell it as a dormant layer; a mask_raw there would hide the one fact the config exists
// to express. (The plan's Task-6 table over-prescribes `mask_raw $1E` here. Derive it.)
//
// Both bridges are SIGNED (i16) precisely because -1 is the "derive" sentinel — a u8/u16
// field cannot hold it, and a 0 sentinel is unusable here since 0 is a legal value for
// both. Neither is a feature: normalising the two shipped records away is a post-P1
// cleanup that costs a deliberate repin/refreeze, and until then a bridge that reproduces
// the authored byte beats a model that quietly changes it. A THIRD bridge species would
// be a spec reconciliation, not an addition — none was needed for the 20 shipped configs
// (nine independently byte-verified through this lowering at review).
//
// no hand-building: use scene() (every guard lives there). A literal `Scene{ .. }`
// type-checks and bypasses the pad guard, the mask-superset guard and both ported anchor
// guards in one go — same convention as SceneLayer above and
// raster_dsl.emp:90.
// -----------------------------------------------
pub struct Scene {
    sc_layers:             [SceneLayer; 16], // padded to MAX_PARALLAX_BANDS; sc_count says how many are real
    sc_count:              u8,
    sc_v_factor:           u8,               // whole-plane Plane-B vshift (pcfg_v_factor_bg)
    sc_v_center:           u16,             // WORLD Y, 0 .. $7FFF (scene() bounds it; the camera never leaves that span)
    sc_v_offset:           i16,             // SIGNED scroll word, -32768 .. 32767 — scene_hdr() two's-complement encodes it into the u16 pcfg_v_offset
    sc_v_factor_fg:        u8,               // RESERVED in the v1 runtime; authored for identity
    sc_deform_fg:          SceneDeform,
    sc_deform_bg:          SceneDeform,
    sc_v_deform:           SceneVDeform,
    sc_anchor:             SceneAnchor,
    // P3 TASK 12 — the mandatory left-column policy for a per-column-VSRAM scene.
    // Comptime-only like every attachment here: Factor0Lock and Accept lower to NOTHING
    // (the record is unchanged — one is a verified impossibility claim, the other a
    // decision to ship), and SpriteMask is refused by scene() until its engine emission
    // lands (see the SceneLeftColMask banner). NO FIELD DEFAULT on purpose: the two
    // back-door `Scene{ .. }` literals in games/sonic4/test/poison must spell
    // Undeclared themselves, the same way they spell every other attachment's None.
    sc_left_col_mask:      SceneLeftColMask,
    sc_transition:         u8,               // TRANS_SMOOTH / TRANS_INSTANT
    // Pad so the two i16 bridges below land on EVEN offsets. `Scene` is comptime-only —
    // it never reaches ROM, so an odd 2-byte field could not fault a 68000 today, and
    // sigil's [layout.odd-field] is a false positive here in the strict sense. Padded
    // anyway, for one cheap reason: the day anything emits a Scene, an odd word field is
    // an address error, and a warning that has been explained away in a baseline is not
    // there to catch it. Zero cost — this struct emits nothing either way.
    //
    // THIS PAD'S WIDTH IS NOT SELF-EVIDENT AND MUST NOT BE TRUSTED BY EYE. It is a
    // function of every field above it, so any insertion up there silently re-parities
    // the two bridges — which is exactly what happened between 2026-08-18 and 2026-08-22:
    // the pad was hand-computed for offsets 94/96, four commits added fields above it, and
    // the bridges drifted to an odd 119/121 with [layout.odd-field] firing into a warning
    // baseline nobody re-read. The AUTHORITY on this width is the `(align: 2)` on the two
    // bridges below (error-tier, per-field, not a lint — no `@allow` reaches it; sigil
    // crates/sigil-frontend-emp/tests/eval_layout.rs:376-444), not any number written here
    // or in the field's name. If it fires, widen or narrow this field by one byte until it
    // passes again.
    //
    // STILL HAND-COMPUTED. `(align: N)` ASSERTS the property; it does not compute the pad
    // (sigil spec 2 §4.3: the compiler never inserts alignment or padding, because an
    // auto-aligning struct would silently break byte-exact ports). A deriving `pad_to(N)`
    // is drafted-but-unlanded in sigil — until it ships, this width is a guarded constant,
    // not a derived one (docs/DEFERRED_WORK.md "THE CLASS" / "ALIGN").
    // (u16 -> u8 on 2026-08-26 when sc_precision, one byte above it, was retired.)
    sc_pad_5D:             u8 = 0,
    // The bridges' parity is STRUCTURAL: `(align: 2)` fails the build naming the field,
    // the claim and the live offset the moment anything above re-parities them (spelling
    // per sigil 6fae4d6a — `@align(N)` is the `vars` cursor-mover and is refused here by
    // name). Scene is comptime-only TODAY, so nothing faults yet; this guards the day
    // something emits one, when a move.w to an odd word field is a 68000 address error.
    // i16 -> i32 ON 2026-08-27, AND THIS IS THE SENTINEL COLLISION THE OLD COMMENT
    // PREDICTED, ARRIVING. `-1` means "derive". At an 8-bit mask the widest legal raw value
    // was $FF, comfortably clear of the sentinel. At a 16-bit mask $FFFF is not an exotic
    // value — it is exactly the all-sixteen-enabled mask an author writes — and it
    // round-trips through an i16 to -1 and reads back as "derive", SILENTLY, at the
    // `mask_raw >= 0` test in scene_hdr(). Widening the range guard alone would have
    // re-armed precisely the failure the guard's own comment warned about.
    //
    // WIDENING THE FIELD, NOT MOVING THE SENTINEL, and the reason is that -1 has to stay
    // OUTSIDE the value space rather than merely outside the values seen so far. An i32
    // holds every 16-bit mask AND -1 with 15 bits of daylight between them, so the next
    // ceiling raise (a 32-bit mask would need MAX_PARALLAX_BANDS = 32, itself far past the
    // 224-line shadow view) inherits a bridge that still cannot collide. A re-spelled
    // sentinel — say $10000, or a companion "is authored" flag — would have worked too and
    // was rejected as strictly more moving parts for the same guarantee: the flag adds a
    // second field two constructors must keep consistent, and a magic non-negative sentinel
    // makes every `>= 0` reader wrong instead of every `== -1` reader.
    //
    // `(align: 2)` AND NOT 4: the claim exists so that the day something EMITS a Scene an
    // odd word/long field is not a 68000 address error, and a 68000 long access requires an
    // EVEN address, not a multiple of four. Widening this field by two bytes shifts
    // sc_v_deform_shift_raw by two and therefore does not re-parity it; sc_pad_5D's
    // hand-computed width is likewise untouched, because sc_layers grew by 8 x
    // sizeof(SceneLayer), and eight of anything is even.
    sc_mask_raw:           i32 (align: 2),   // -1 = derive from ly_enabled (bridge, see above)
    sc_v_deform_shift_raw: i16 (align: 2),   // -1 = derive from sc_v_deform (bridge, see above)
}

// -----------------------------------------------
// scene — the authored scene constructor.
//
// Ports hdr()'s two load-bearing guards BY NAME (spec §3.1) plus the layer-count
// ceiling. All three compare INTS, extracted through the accessors above, for the
// reason stated in their banner.
//
// The `layers` array is always sixteen elements; pad with no_layer(). `count` is the
// authority on how many are real — sc_layers is never scanned past it.
// -----------------------------------------------
pub comptime fn scene(layers: [SceneLayer; 16], count: int,
                      v_factor: int, v_center: int = 0, v_offset: int = 0,
                      v_factor_fg: int = 0,
                      deform_fg: SceneDeform = SceneDeform.None,
                      deform_bg: SceneDeform = SceneDeform.None,
                      v_deform: SceneVDeform = SceneVDeform.None,
                      anchor: SceneAnchor = SceneAnchor.None,
                      left_column_mask: SceneLeftColMask = SceneLeftColMask.Undeclared,
                      transition: int = 0,
                      layer_mask_raw: int = -1,
                      v_deform_shift_raw: int = -1) -> Scene {
    // 16 = MAX_PARALLAX_BANDS, 0 = TRANS_SMOOTH: inlined per the pin block at the top of
    // this file (the first is imported and would not travel; the other is a default,
    // evaluated wherever the call site puts it).
    ensure(count >= 1 && count <= 16,
           "scene(): {count} layers exceeds MAX_PARALLAX_BANDS (16) — the shadow view is sized for sixteen entries and a seventeenth writes past Parallax_Shadow_Bands")
    let anchor_ch = scene_anchor_ch(anchor)
    // Ported hdr() guard 1: an anchored scene SPLITS a layer at runtime, so the shadow
    // view needs count+1 entries. Same reasoning, same ceiling, same failure mode as
    // configs.emp's hdr(); this is one of the two pairs Task 7 proves equal.
    ensure(anchor_ch == $FF || count + 1 <= 16,
           "scene(): an anchored scene SPLITS a layer at runtime, so the shadow view needs count+1 entries — {count}+1 exceeds MAX_PARALLAX_BANDS (16) and the split would write past Parallax_Shadow_Bands")
    // Ported hdr() guard 2: the anchor channel must index the patch bank.
    ensure(anchor_ch == $FF || anchor_ch < 4,
           "scene(): anchor ch {anchor_ch} is not a patch channel — the overlay indexes Effects_World_Y[RASTER_MAX_PATCH] (4), so a wider index reads past the anchor bank")
    // THE PAD GUARD. `count` is the sole authority on how many layers are real, and both
    // folds (derived_mask and scene_band) stop there — so a fifth authored layer under
    // `count: 4` is lowered by nothing AND masked by nothing. Silent, and invisible until
    // a word-level compare. This closes it.
    //
    // BOTH CONJUNCTS ARE REQUIRED. `ly_enabled == 0` alone would reject a legal shipped
    // shape: OJZ_LockedClouds (configs.emp:299) is five real layers with layer 0 dormant,
    // so a masked-off REAL layer inside the count must keep passing. `ly_fa == 0` is what
    // separates the two — no authored layer can have it (see no_layer()'s note).
    for i in count..16 {
        let p = layers[i]
        ensure(p.ly_enabled == 0 && p.ly_fa == 0 && p.ly_fb == 0,
               "scene(): slot {i} is past count {count} but is not a no_layer() pad — count is the authority on how many layers are real, and a real layer beyond it is lowered by nothing and masked by nothing")
    }
    // THE MASK BRIDGE IS A SUPERSET, NEVER A CONTRADICTION. sc_mask_raw exists to ADD
    // bits the model cannot derive (OJZ_Default: $1F over a derived $0F, which passes),
    // not to CLEAR one that ly_enabled set. Clearing is an authoring contradiction with a
    // correct spelling one field over — `enabled: 0` on that layer — and left unguarded it
    // is a mask the lowering emits while the model says otherwise.
    ensure(layer_mask_raw < 0 || (layer_mask_raw | derived_mask(layers, count)) == layer_mask_raw,
           "scene(): layer_mask_raw {layer_mask_raw} CLEARS a bit the enabled layers set — the raw mask is a byte-identity bridge that may only ADD bits the model cannot derive; to disable a layer, author it with enabled: 0")
    // Width AND sentinel in one range. pcfg_layer_mask is a u16 since 2026-08-27
    // (engine/structs.emp) — one bit per band at MAX_PARALLAX_BANDS = 16 — so the width
    // half is now $FFFF. THE SENTINEL HALF IS THE INTERESTING ONE AND IT MOVED: while the
    // mask was 8 bits, the i16 bridge field could not collide with -1 because $FFFF was not
    // a legal mask. It is now the all-enabled mask, so the bridge field was widened to i32
    // (see Scene.sc_mask_raw's note). Without that widening this guard would admit $FFFF,
    // the field would store -1, and scene_hdr()'s `mask_raw >= 0` would silently DERIVE
    // instead of bridging. Any negative other than -1 is still caught here for the original
    // reason — it would also read as "derive".
    ensure(layer_mask_raw >= -1 && layer_mask_raw <= $FFFF,
           "scene(): layer_mask_raw {layer_mask_raw} outside -1 (derive) .. $FFFF — pcfg_layer_mask is a u16 (one bit per band at MAX_PARALLAX_BANDS 16), and a wider value would wrap through the i32 bridge field")
    // Same shape, same reason. The runtime samples with `asr.w d3, d5` (parallax.emp:1038),
    // so a shift past 15 saturates to the sign bit: a wrong picture, not a fault, which is
    // exactly the class of error that belongs to the build.
    ensure(v_deform_shift_raw >= -1 && v_deform_shift_raw <= 15,
           "scene(): v_deform_shift_raw {v_deform_shift_raw} outside -1 (derive) .. 15 — the runtime samples the column table with asr.w, so a wider shift saturates to sign instead of failing")
    // ---- THE WHOLE-PLANE VERTICAL SHIFT'S RANGE, which had no owner ----
    //
    // THE BOUND IS DERIVED FROM THE ONE CONSUMER, not from the sentinel. sc_v_factor
    // lowers to pcfg_v_factor_bg, and `Parallax_Step5_Vscroll` does exactly two things
    // with that byte: it compares it against 15, and otherwise uses it as the count of
    // an `asr.w d2, d0`. So 15 is CLAIMED as the lock sentinel and is not a shift; and
    // `asr.w` takes its register count mod 64, with every count from 15 up filling the
    // word with its sign bit — which is why 15 was available to be the sentinel in the
    // first place, and is the same fact scene_plane_line's banner states as
    // "(wy - v_center) >> 15 is 0 or -1 for every wy". That leaves 0..14 as the shifts
    // the runtime can distinguish, 15 as the lock, and 16..255 as values that neither
    // lock nor shift: `.v_locked` is not taken, the dividend collapses to 0/-1, and the
    // plane is silently wrong. Identical class to v_deform_shift_raw above, identical
    // reason to refuse it here.
    //
    // THE LOWER HALF IS NOT DECORATION. `scene()` takes an `int` and sc_v_factor is a
    // u8, so `v_factor: -1` would wrap to 255 — the SAME defect through a different
    // door — and this guard elaborates before the Scene{ .. } construction, so it is
    // the only thing that can see the negative.
    //
    // THE DEFECT THAT PROMPTED IT (VFACTOR): Aurora's editor default for a new scene is
    // the string `"FACTOR_0"`, which is parallax_dsl's PACKED HORIZONTAL encoding
    // (FACTOR_LOCKED = $0FF = 255), not a raw shift. tools/effects_gen.py now refuses
    // the STRING as a shape error; this refuses the NUMBER, and the two halves are
    // deliberately separate — a generator that grew a range check would be a second
    // source for this rule.
    ensure(v_factor >= 0 && v_factor <= 15,
           "scene(): v_factor {v_factor} outside 0 .. 15 — Parallax_Step5_Vscroll reads this byte as `asr.w d2, d0` unless it equals 15, which is the lock sentinel, and asr.w takes its count mod 64 with everything from 15 up filling the word with its sign. So a value above 15 does NOT lock and does not shift either: the BG plane silently pins to 0/-1 instead of tracking the camera. 0..14 are the shifts, 15 is the lock. (If this fired on 255, suspect a PACKED FACTOR_* value in a raw-shift field — FACTOR_0 is $0FF and the two namespaces share this field's name.)")
    // v_factor_fg IS BOUNDED SEPARATELY AND THE DERIVATION IS NOT THE SAME ONE, even
    // though it lands on the same two numbers. It lowers to pcfg_v_factor_fg, which
    // structs.emp marks RESERVED and which NOTHING READS — Step 5 sets the FG vscroll to
    // camY unconditionally — so there is no consumer to derive a bound from, and no
    // authored value here can produce a wrong picture today. What is derivable is the
    // field's declared identity: it is the FG twin of v_factor, same type, same name,
    // the argument beside it, and the day the reservation is lifted it becomes the same
    // asr.w count and inherits the same span. So this is a RESERVATION bound, and it is
    // worth having for one reason only: it makes the 255 above impossible on this field
    // BEFORE anything reads it, rather than after. It is deliberately not `== 0` — that
    // would pin the v1 runtime's silence as if it were the field's meaning, and would
    // have to be found and widened by whoever lifts the reservation, which is the
    // staling-constant trap sc_pad_5D already sprang once in this file.
    // ---- THE TWO FIELDS BESIDE v_factor (VOFFSET parcel), and the two bounds are NOT the
    // same kind of number, which is why the field types differ.
    //
    // v_offset IS SIGNED. Its only consumers are `add.w pcfg_v_offset(a0), d0` on the unlocked
    // arm and `move.w pcfg_v_offset(a0), d2` on `.v_locked`, both landing in
    // Parallax_Current_Vscroll_BG, which the transition lerp then treats with sub.w/asr.w —
    // signed-word arithmetic end to end, producing a scroll word the VDP reads modulo the
    // plane span. A negative offset is therefore a meaningful authoring value that the 16-bit
    // add wraps exactly as the hardware does (Aurora's `v_offset: -8` is the booking's own
    // example), and the bound is the signed word. It was a `u16` here and in the config, so
    // -8 was refused by the EMITTER — `[emit.out-of-range] -8 does not fit u16`, pointing at
    // generated code — for a value that was never wrong. sc_v_offset is now an i16 and
    // scene_hdr() encodes it into the u16 config word; this guard is what makes that encode
    // total.
    ensure(v_offset >= -32768 && v_offset <= 32767,
           "scene(): v_offset {v_offset} outside -32768 .. 32767 — Parallax_Step5_Vscroll adds this field to the shifted camera term with a 16-bit `add.w` (and the `.v_locked` arm copies it straight into Vscroll_BG), so it is a SIGNED scroll word: a negative offset is legal and wraps exactly as the hardware does, but a value past either end of the word cannot be carried by the field at all. Author the offset as a signed pixel count")
    // v_center IS A WORLD Y, and world Y is the space layer() already bounds world_y to, by
    // the same derivation: the camera is clamped to max(0, min(y, extent - screen)) with every
    // act descriptor asserting extent <= $8000, so no camera position is ever negative or past
    // $7FFF. Step 5 subtracts this field from the camera Y with a signed-word `sub.w`, and
    // scene_plane_line() compares layer tops against it in the same space (`wy >= vc`), so a
    // value in $8000..$FFFF would fit the u16 and READ as a negative Y the camera can never sit
    // at, and a negative one cannot be stored at all. Deliberately NOT tightened to this game's
    // act span (SCENE_ACT_SPAN_Y): a v_center beyond the act but under $7FFF is legal-but-odd
    // and is the registry's question, exactly as it is for world_y.
    ensure(v_center >= 0 && v_center <= 32767,
           "scene(): v_center {v_center} outside 0 .. 32767 — this is a WORLD Y (the camera Y the mapping ((camY - v_center) >> v_factor) + v_offset is centred on), and the camera is clamped to [0, act extent] with every act extent asserted <= $8000, so no camera position is ever negative or past $7FFF; Parallax_Step5_Vscroll subtracts it with a signed-word `sub.w`, so a value at or past $8000 would silently read as a NEGATIVE Y. Same span layer() gives world_y; a v_center beyond THIS act's span is legal and is the registry's question")
    ensure(v_factor_fg >= 0 && v_factor_fg <= 15,
           "scene(): v_factor_fg {v_factor_fg} outside 0 .. 15 — this is v_factor's FG twin and takes v_factor's span. The v1 runtime does not READ it (pcfg_v_factor_fg is RESERVED; Step 5 always sets the FG vscroll to camY), so a wider value cannot bend a picture today — it would sit in the emitted record as a number with no meaning, waiting for the parcel that lifts the reservation to read it as a shift. Author 0 unless you are reproducing a byte")
    // THE SELECTOR IS RANGE-CHECKED AS AN ARGUMENT, not just pinned as a constant. It is
    // stored raw and read by a one-bit test, so an out-of-range value does not fail, it
    // MEANS something: `transition: 2` is tested with tst.b/bne (parallax.emp:259) and
    // therefore silently means INSTANT.
    ensure(transition == 0 || transition == 1,
           "scene(): transition {transition} is neither TRANS_SMOOTH (0) nor TRANS_INSTANT (1) — the runtime tests this byte with tst.b, so anything nonzero silently means instant")
    // ---- P3 TASK 9: the two scene-level guards on the per-layer deform ref ----
    //
    // (1) POSITION, the mirror of layer()'s. `Own` is a per-LAYER attachment: its table
    //     and speed lower into a BAND's extension record, and a scene-level slot has no
    //     band to put them in — the plane header (pcfg_deform_table_fg/bg) is one table
    //     for the whole config. Refused by VARIANT, never by payload.
    ensure(scene_deform_is_own(deform_fg) == 0 && scene_deform_is_own(deform_bg) == 0,
           "scene(): deform: Own(..) is a PER-LAYER attachment — pass it to layer(deform:). A scene-level slot lowers to the plane's pcfg_deform_table_* header word, which is ONE table for the whole config and has nowhere to put a per-layer speed. For a scene-wide table use SceneDeform.Shared(table, speed)")
    // (2) AN OWN() LAYER DOES NOT REPLACE THE SCENE'S TABLE, IT OVERRIDES IT. own()
    //     overrides the plane-shared table FOR ONE LAYER, and the layers that did not
    //     override it still need something to sample: Parallax_Fill_PerLine loads the
    //     header's two table words once (`cap_deform_tables`) and only an own() band
    //     reloads a5/a6 from its extension record, so with both header words null every
    //     non-own band with a live shift would sample from address 0. Requiring a
    //     scene-level table on at least one plane is the honest model of the mechanism.
    //
    //     HISTORY: until 2026-08-26 this guard's stated reason was the RUNTIME MODE KEY —
    //     `parallax_mode_key()` decided per-line vs per-cell by ORing the two header
    //     table words and could not see the bands, so an own()-only scene would have
    //     answered per-cell at runtime while the lowering answered per-line. That key and
    //     the per-cell path are deleted (d-29-corrected); the sampling reason above is the
    //     one that survives, and it is sufficient on its own.
    let any_own = derived_own(layers, count)
    // Flat accumulator, never a nested if-expression (docs/EMP_PITFALLS.md §1).
    comptime var any_scene_table = 0
    if scene_deform_is_none(deform_fg) == 0 {
        any_scene_table = 1
    }
    if scene_deform_is_none(deform_bg) == 0 {
        any_scene_table = 1
    }
    ensure(any_own == 0 || any_scene_table == 1,
           "scene(): a layer attaches deform: Own(..) but this scene attaches NO plane-shared table on either plane. own() OVERRIDES the scene's table for one layer; it cannot be the only table, because Parallax_Fill_PerLine loads the config's two HEADER table words once and only an own() band reloads them from its extension record — with both header words null every other band with a live shift would sample from address 0. Attach the plane-shared table the other layers sample (deform_fg:/deform_bg: SceneDeform.Shared(..))")
    // ---- P3 TASK 10: the one scene-level guard on curves ----
    //
    // THE ANCHORED OVERLAY WRITES DEFORM SHIFTS INTO BANDS IT DOES NOT OWN, and that is
    // what makes this a SCENE-level question rather than a layer-level one. Step 4b
    // overrides `band_deform_shift_a/b` with pcfg_anchor_dsa/dsb in EVERY band from the
    // split down — so an anchored scene with live anchor shifts turns deform on for bands
    // whose layer authored none, and a curve layer down there would become curve ∧ deform
    // at RUNTIME, past every comptime guard. The fill resolves the collision by testing the
    // curve first (a curve band never samples), which would silently drop the anchor's
    // deform on exactly those rows. Refusing the combination is the honest half.
    //
    // CURVES AND ANCHORS STILL COMPOSE — with a PURE-BOUNDARY anchor (both shifts 15),
    // which is design §2's own case: "an anchor split inside a curve layer CONTINUES the
    // curve". That path is live, and the continuation is a real mechanism rather than a
    // tautology: without it the split would re-parametrise the ramp at the boundary. See
    // parallax.emp's CURVE_FLAG_CONT_BIT.
    let any_curve = derived_curve(layers, count)
    comptime var anchor_amp = 0
    if anchor_ch != $FF {
        if scene_anchor_dsa(anchor) != 15 {
            anchor_amp = 1
        }
        if scene_anchor_dsb(anchor) != 15 {
            anchor_amp = 1
        }
    }
    ensure(any_curve == 0 || anchor_amp == 0,
           "scene(): this scene carries a curve layer AND an anchor with live deform shifts (anchor dsa {scene_anchor_dsa(anchor)} / dsb {scene_anchor_dsb(anchor)}; 15 is the no-deform sentinel). The overlay writes those shifts into every band from the split DOWN, including bands whose layer authored no deform — so a curve layer below the split would be curve ∧ deform at runtime, which design §2 forbids and the fill's register allocation cannot serve. A PURE-BOUNDARY anchor (dsa 15, dsb 15) composes with curves and is design §2's own case")
    // ---- P3 TASK 11: THE TWO-WRITER RULING, and why both halves are SCENE-level ----
    //
    // Plane B's vertical scroll word is written by `Parallax_Step5_Vscroll`/`Vscroll_Write`
    // in VBlank and by a vscroll-split fire mid-frame. Which of those two is a COLLISION and
    // which is a legal precedence is decided by two SCENE fields — `v_factor` and
    // `v_deform` — neither of which `layer()` can see, so this is where the ruling lives.
    // The full statement is in the SceneVSplit banner above; these are its two refusals.
    //
    // THEY SIT IN scene(), NOT IN THE FOLD, and that is deliberate: a scene that authors a
    // vsplit and never calls `scene_vsplit_fires()` would otherwise carry an unadjudicated
    // collision, and "the guard is in the lowering nobody called" is exactly how the
    // reachability lessons in this file's banner were learned. Every scene is constructed;
    // not every scene is lowered.
    //
    // 15 is the lock sentinel, inlined per the pin block at the top of this file.
    let any_vsplit = derived_vsplit(layers, count)
    ensure(any_vsplit == 0 || v_factor == 15,
           "scene(): a layer authors vsplit: At(..) while this scene's Plane-B vertical scroll TRACKS THE CAMERA (v_factor {v_factor}; 15 is the lock sentinel). That is the two-writer collision: Parallax_Step5_Vscroll recomputes ((camY - v_center) >> v_factor) + v_offset every VBlank and Vscroll_Write ships it to VSRAM entry 1 at frame top, while the lowered split writes an ABSOLUTE constant to the same word mid-frame. The two do not merely disagree about a value — the split cannot express what the other writer is for: it carries ONE baked scroll value at ONE baked fire line, and that line is derived at comptime from the layer top, which is a screen line only while Vscroll_BG is constant. Lock the plane (v_factor: 15) and author the depth as a split, or express it horizontally (layer(fb:) / curve:), which the walker recomputes every frame")
    ensure(any_vsplit == 0 || scene_vdeform_is_none(v_deform) == 1,
           "scene(): a layer authors vsplit: At(..) while this scene attaches a per-column V-deform table (SceneVDeform.Columns). Same word, second collision: in per-column mode (VDP reg $0B bit 2) VSRAM entry 1 is PLANE B OF COLUMN 0, not the plane, and Vscroll_Write ships the whole 80-byte column buffer by DMA each frame — so a whole-plane mid-frame write below the line would shift ONE 16-px column of forty and leave the rest where the column buffer put them. Whole-plane vertical depth and per-column V-deform are two spellings of the same VSRAM; author one of them")
    // ---- P3 TASK 12: THE LEFT-COLUMN POLICY, mandatory where the artifact is possible ----
    //
    // The artifact, the repair that removed it, what each arm means now and the SpriteMask
    // refusal are all documented at the SceneLeftColMask banner; these guards are the
    // enforcement. They live in scene()
    // for the vsplit ruling's reason: every scene is constructed, not every scene is
    // lowered, and a per-column scene must not exist unadjudicated. The failing ensure
    // elaborates at the constructor call, so the error's source line IS the scene.
    let lcm_undeclared = scene_lcm_is_undeclared(left_column_mask)
    let vdeform_on     = 1 - scene_vdeform_is_none(v_deform)
    // (1) MANDATORY. A per-column scene without a declared policy fails the build. The
    //     message carries the scene's authored signature (count / v-deform speed / shift)
    //     because sigil reports a comptime-fn ensure at the ENSURE's own span, not the
    //     call site — the interpolated values are what lets the author tell Rocking_Slow
    //     (1 layer, speed 0, shift 0) from Perspective_Subtle (5 layers, speed 0, shift 2).
    ensure(vdeform_on == 0 || lcm_undeclared == 0,
           "scene(): a scene with {count} layer(s) attaching a per-column V-deform table (SceneVDeform.Columns, sample speed {scene_vdeform_speed(v_deform)}, amplitude shift {scene_vdeform_shift(v_deform)}) declares NO left_column_mask policy. Per-column V-scroll makes a plane's leading sliver render at VSRAM[$4C] & VSRAM[$4E] instead of its own column-0 entry; the engine REPAIRS that unconditionally (the column-19 borrow in Parallax_Step5_Vscroll writes plane A's V-scroll into that pair's plane-B word, so the AND is the foreground's own scroll), and the repair costs plane B 16 px at the RIGHT edge, where the same entry renders. So the declaration is no longer about the left column — it is the author saying they have seen that price: declare left_column_mask: SceneLeftColMask.Accept (accept the residual; both shipped per-column families spell it) or .Factor0Lock (verified: NEITHER plane can H-scroll, so this scene has no sliver at all). .SpriteMask is ruled out — see the SceneLeftColMask banner in engine/level/scene_dsl.emp and docs/research/2026-08-29-vsram-column19-borrow.md")
    // (2) NO POLICY WITHOUT A SUBJECT. On a scene with no per-column V-deform there is
    //     neither an artifact nor a repair to price, so a declared policy is a claim about
    //     nothing — and a reader who finds one will believe the scene uses per-column
    //     VSRAM. Refused as noise.
    ensure(vdeform_on == 1 || lcm_undeclared == 1,
           "scene(): left_column_mask is declared but this scene attaches NO per-column V-deform table — the leftmost-partial-column artifact is a property of per-column VSRAM mode (VDP reg $0B bit 2), which this scene never enters, so the policy adjudicates an artifact that cannot occur. Remove the declaration; it would only mislead the next reader into believing this scene is a per-column one")
    // (3) FACTOR0LOCK IS A VERIFIED CLAIM, ON BOTH PLANES, or it is not available.
    //
    //     WHY BOTH. The variant certifies "the leftmost partial column CANNOT EXIST",
    //     and that is a PER-PLANE fact. Per-column V-scroll (VDP reg $0B bit 2) is one
    //     bit for the whole screen, but the sliver appears on a plane exactly when THAT
    //     plane's HScroll is not a multiple of the 16-px V-scroll grain, and the two
    //     planes' HScroll words are independent sums: Decode_Factor_A/B (the same
    //     routine over different record bytes) plus that plane's own deform sample
    //     (band_deform_shift_a/b against band_table_a/b). A locked plane B therefore
    //     says nothing about plane A.
    //
    //     UNTIL 2026-08-28 THESE GUARDS SCANNED `fb`/`dsb` ONLY, and the omission was
    //     not cosmetic: a scene could lock plane B, declare Factor0Lock, pass the build,
    //     and still lose its leftmost 16 px on plane A. Nothing shipped was
    //     mis-certified — both per-column families spell Accept — but the build would
    //     have ADMITTED the false claim, which is the "gate certifies the plane nobody
    //     is looking at" failure. Derivation and the measured signature:
    //     docs/research/2026-08-28-deform-scene-left-edge-triage.md.
    //
    //     CONSEQUENCE, STATED SO IT IS NOT REDISCOVERED AS A BUG: a side-scrolling
    //     scene's foreground tracks the camera (`fa: FACTOR_1`), so the lock is FALSE on
    //     plane A for essentially every real scene, and Factor0Lock is out of reach for
    //     them. That is the honest reading of the mechanism, not a guard to loosen. The
    //     two live answers for a scrolling per-column scene are Accept and the unbuilt
    //     SpriteMask (see the SceneLeftColMask banner).
    //
    //     HALF ONE — THE BASE FACTORS, one ensure per plane so the failure NAMES the
    //     plane. $0FF is inlined per the pin block at the top of this file (FACTOR_0),
    //     which holds it for both planes. The scan covers DORMANT layers too: a disabled
    //     band inherits the PREVIOUS band's scroll words (the walker's .band_disabled
    //     arm), so any non-locked factor inside the count can reach the hardware and the
    //     conservative scan is the correct one.
    //
    //     THE CURVE IS A THIRD SCROLL SOURCE and it is scanned here as well. A
    //     `curve: SceneCurve.To(fb2)` layer RAMPS the effective plane-B factor from its
    //     own `fb` to `fb2` across the layer (parallax.emp's `.lp_curve` writes a moving
    //     BG word under a constant FG one), so an all-FACTOR_0 `fb` scan passes while
    //     plane-B HScroll leaves zero on every line below the layer top. Curves are BG
    //     only by design (SceneCurve's banner: plane A is hard-locked to -Camera_X and
    //     never lerped), so there is no plane-A twin of this one.
    let lcm_f0 = scene_lcm_is_factor0_lock(left_column_mask)
    // Flat accumulators over STATEMENT ifs, never nested if-expressions
    // (docs/EMP_PITFALLS.md §1).
    comptime var lcm_fa_unlocked    = 0
    comptime var lcm_fb_unlocked    = 0
    comptime var lcm_curve_unlocked = 0
    for i in 0..count {
        let l = layers[i]
        if l.ly_fa != $0FF {
            lcm_fa_unlocked = 1
        }
        if l.ly_fb != $0FF {
            lcm_fb_unlocked = 1
        }
        if scene_curve_is_none(l.ly_curve) == 0 {
            if scene_curve_to(l.ly_curve) != $0FF {
                lcm_curve_unlocked = 1
            }
        }
    }
    ensure(lcm_f0 == 0 || lcm_fa_unlocked == 0,
           "scene(): left_column_mask: Factor0Lock claims NEITHER plane can H-scroll, but PLANE A is not locked — a real layer's fa is not FACTOR_0. Plane A's HScroll is its own sum (Decode_Factor_A), and the leftmost partial column appears on a plane whenever THAT plane's HScroll is not a multiple of the 16-px V-scroll grain, so locking plane B does not save the foreground: it is the foreground strip the artifact eats. In a side-scrolling scene fa tracks the camera (FACTOR_1) and this claim is simply not available — lock every layer's fa to FACTOR_0 as well, or declare Accept. NOTE what this arm does and does not buy since the column-19 borrow: the engine repairs the sliver either way, so Factor0Lock now asserts only that this scene never HAD one; it does not exempt the scene from the borrow's right-edge cost")
    ensure(lcm_f0 == 0 || lcm_fb_unlocked == 0,
           "scene(): left_column_mask: Factor0Lock claims NEITHER plane can H-scroll, but PLANE B is not locked — a real layer's fb is not FACTOR_0. The leftmost partial column exists on plane B on every line where its HScroll is non-zero, so this claim is false as authored. Lock every layer's fb to FACTOR_0, or declare Accept")
    ensure(lcm_f0 == 0 || lcm_curve_unlocked == 0,
           "scene(): left_column_mask: Factor0Lock claims NEITHER plane can H-scroll, but a layer RAMPS its plane-B factor — curve: SceneCurve.To(..) with a far end that is not FACTOR_0. The ramp is a scroll source the fb scan cannot see: every fb can be locked while the curve walks plane-B HScroll away from zero across the layer, and the sliver comes back on those lines. Ramp to FACTOR_0, drop the curve, or declare Accept")
    //     HALF TWO — THE DEFORM AMPLITUDES, again one ensure per plane. Deform adds
    //     per-line HScroll ON TOP of the locked factor (the fill's sample loop writes
    //     fg|bg words from band_table_a/b under band_deform_shift_a/b), so a locked base
    //     under a live dsa/dsb brings the sliver back on exactly those rows —
    //     Perspective's shimmer floor is the shipped plane-B example, and both shipped
    //     families carry `dsa: 4` over an FG table, which is the plane-A one.
    //     The test is CONSERVATIVE BY CONSTRUCTION on both planes and that is a
    //     documented cost, not an oversight: table CONTENTS are Labels, invisible at
    //     comptime, so a live shift against the all-zero table (Rocking) is
    //     indistinguishable from a live one against a real table and is refused with the
    //     rest. Rocking spells Accept for exactly this reason.
    comptime var lcm_a_amp = 0
    comptime var lcm_b_amp = 0
    for i in 0..count {
        let l = layers[i]
        if l.ly_dsa != 15 {
            lcm_a_amp = 1
        }
        if l.ly_dsb != 15 {
            lcm_b_amp = 1
        }
    }
    if anchor_ch != $FF {
        if scene_anchor_dsa(anchor) != 15 {
            lcm_a_amp = 1
        }
        if scene_anchor_dsb(anchor) != 15 {
            lcm_b_amp = 1
        }
    }
    // A table that can reach a plane: that plane's scene-level attachment (deform_fg for
    // A, deform_bg for B — band_table_a/b resolve exactly this fallback), or any layer's
    // own() table, which serves BOTH planes (see SceneDeform's banner).
    comptime var lcm_a_table = 0
    if scene_deform_is_none(deform_fg) == 0 {
        lcm_a_table = 1
    }
    if any_own == 1 {
        lcm_a_table = 1
    }
    comptime var lcm_b_table = 0
    if scene_deform_is_none(deform_bg) == 0 {
        lcm_b_table = 1
    }
    if any_own == 1 {
        lcm_b_table = 1
    }
    comptime var lcm_a_deform_live = 0
    if lcm_a_amp == 1 {
        if lcm_a_table == 1 {
            lcm_a_deform_live = 1
        }
    }
    comptime var lcm_b_deform_live = 0
    if lcm_b_amp == 1 {
        if lcm_b_table == 1 {
            lcm_b_deform_live = 1
        }
    }
    ensure(lcm_f0 == 0 || lcm_a_deform_live == 0,
           "scene(): left_column_mask: Factor0Lock claims the leftmost partial column cannot exist, but this scene can put live H-deform on PLANE A — a layer or anchor dsa is not 15 while a table can reach that plane (the scene's deform_fg, or a layer's Own table, which serves both planes). Deform adds per-line plane-A HScroll on top of the locked factor, so the sliver comes back on the FOREGROUND on exactly those rows. Same conservatism as the plane-B half: table contents are Labels and invisible at comptime, so a live dsa against an all-zero table is refused too. Silence the plane-A amplitude, or declare Accept")
    ensure(lcm_f0 == 0 || lcm_b_deform_live == 0,
           "scene(): left_column_mask: Factor0Lock claims the leftmost partial column cannot exist, but this scene can put live H-deform on PLANE B — a layer or anchor dsb is not 15 while a table can reach that plane (the scene's deform_bg, or a layer's Own table, which serves both planes). Deform adds per-line plane-B HScroll on top of the locked factor, so the sliver comes back on exactly those rows. NOTE the check is conservative: table contents are Labels and invisible at comptime, so a live dsb against the all-zero table is refused too (Rocking spells Accept for this reason). Silence the plane-B amplitude, or declare Accept")
    // (4) SPRITEMASK IS REFUSED, AND SINCE d-40 THE REASON IS A RULING, NOT A BLOCKER.
    //     The owner was offered the bar, the repair and acceptance, and rejected the bar.
    //     The emission is cancelled rather than pending, and the borrow removed the thing
    //     it would have covered — so this ensure is now permanent for as long as the arm
    //     exists, and the arm's own retirement is booked at the SceneLeftColMask banner.
    //     It was already refused before the ruling, for the older reason that an accepted
    //     declaration whose mechanism does not exist would ship the artifact while the
    //     build claimed it was covered; nothing was ever mis-certified through it.
    ensure(scene_lcm_is_sprite_mask(left_column_mask) == 0,
           "scene(): left_column_mask: SpriteMask is declared, and it is RULED OUT — the owner rejected covering the strip and asked for it to be repaired instead (d-40, 2026-08-29). The strip emission is cancelled, not pending: since the column-19 borrow the sliver renders the foreground's own V-scroll on every per-column scene, so there is nothing left for a sprite bar to cover. Declare Factor0Lock or Accept. The never-built emitter's two rulings survive as knowledge only, in docs/DEFERRED_WORK.md §'Sprite mask for per-column V-scroll' (opaque strip at screen X 0 first in the link chain — NOT the VDP's X=0 sprite-masking feature, which suppresses later sprites and cannot repaint a plane pixel; and 7 SAT entries at 8x32, not 1)")
    return Scene{
        sc_layers:             layers,
        sc_count:              count,
        sc_v_factor:           v_factor,
        sc_v_center:           v_center,
        sc_v_offset:           v_offset,
        sc_v_factor_fg:        v_factor_fg,
        sc_deform_fg:          deform_fg,
        sc_deform_bg:          deform_bg,
        sc_v_deform:           v_deform,
        sc_anchor:             anchor,
        sc_left_col_mask:      left_column_mask,
        sc_transition:         transition,
        sc_pad_5D:             default,
        sc_mask_raw:           layer_mask_raw,
        sc_v_deform_shift_raw: v_deform_shift_raw,
    }
}

// =====================================================================
// THE PER-LINE FORCER AND THE RUNTIME MODE KEY LIVED HERE — `scene_forces_per_line()`
// (five comptime arms: any table, an anchor, a curve, `precision: line`, an off-grid top)
// and `parallax_mode_key()` (the runtime half, spliced into engine.parallax's fill select
// and engine.buffers' DMA-length select). Both were deleted 2026-08-26 under owner
// ruling d-29-corrected, together with Parallax_Fill_PerCell, Static_Hscroll_Cell and
// the CAP_PER_LINE bit: with one HScroll fill there is no mode to decide, at comptime or
// at runtime, and the three places that used to decide it (these two plus the reg $0B
// derivation in parallax.emp, which only ever saw the header tables) could disagree —
// they DID, on master, at the first section of act 1. How to get the per-cell path back,
// correctly, is booked in docs/DEFERRED_WORK.md "Per-cell HScroll fill — DELETED".
// =====================================================================

// =====================================================================
// Capability fold (spec §3) — what a scene DEMANDS of the runtime.
//
// CAP_TRANSITIONS IS DELIBERATELY ABSENT HERE, and its absence is the ruling, not an
// omission: the P1 rule is "set when the registry holds more than one scene", and a
// single Scene cannot see the registry's size. The registry-level fold (Task 5) ORs
// scene_caps() over its scenes and adds CAP_TRANSITIONS itself when its count > 1.
// sc_transition is still authored and still lowered — it is the pcfg byte — it just
// does not raise a capability on its own.
//
// THERE IS NO PER-LINE BIT ANY MORE (2026-08-26, d-29-corrected). CAP_PER_LINE ($0001)
// used to be raised by `scene_forces_per_line()`'s five arms and gated the per-line fill
// and its 896-byte DMA; both are unconditional now, so the fold has nothing to say about
// them. Bit 0 is retired, not re-used (see the CAP_* block).
//
// P3 Task 5 PROMOTED two bits (CAP_MULTI_DEFORM_TABLE, CAP_FACTOR_CURVE) to `pub const`
// so their gates can be measured, and nothing raises them yet — Task 7 and Task 10 own
// those lowerings. This fold still does not read them, for the P1 reason: a bit raised
// here without a mechanism behind it is a capability claim with no subject.
//
// The other three folds below (CAP_DEFORM, CAP_PER_COL_VSRAM, CAP_ANCHORS) stay inline —
// each is a single-arm question with no twin, so there is nothing to single-source.
// =====================================================================
pub comptime fn scene_caps(s: Scene) -> int {
    comptime var caps = 0
    // Bit literals, not the CAP_* names: this fn is elaborated at a game call site and
    // only a glob import puts those names there. See the pin block at the top.
    //   $0002 CAP_PER_COL_VSRAM  $0004 CAP_DEFORM  $0008 CAP_ANCHORS  ($0001 retired)
    // NESTED `if`-EXPRESSIONS DO NOT EVALUATE HERE. The first spelling of this was
    // `if a { 1 } else { if b { 1 } else { 0 } }` and it returned 0 whenever only the
    // INNER test was true — measured: a BG-only deform scene folded to caps 0, so
    // OJZ_Default would have shipped claiming it needs nothing per-line. A flat
    // accumulator over statement `if`s is the shape that holds. (A single-level
    // if-expression IS fine; `anchored` below is one.)
    //
    // has_table IS DELIBERATELY PLANE-AGNOSTIC — "is there a table anywhere", not "is
    // there one on the plane that needs it". The pipeline is per-frame and per-line for
    // the whole screen; there is no half-per-line.
    comptime var has_table = 0
    if scene_deform_is_none(s.sc_deform_fg) == 0 {
        has_table = 1
    }
    if scene_deform_is_none(s.sc_deform_bg) == 0 {
        has_table = 1
    }
    let anchored = if scene_anchor_ch(s.sc_anchor) == $FF { 0 } else { 1 }
    // Deform proper needs a table AND a live amplitude somewhere. A table whose every
    // consumer sits at shift 15 is flat-pathed: the sample loop is skipped and the
    // emitted HScroll is identical to the no-deform case, so it demands the per-line
    // pipeline but not the deform math. OJZ_Default is exactly that scene.
    //
    // THE SCAN IS CROSS-PLANE ON PURPOSE — every layer shift and both anchor shifts,
    // regardless of which plane holds a table — and the asymmetry is not a bug to tidy.
    // ParallaxConfig_Haze (configs.emp:181-199) attaches a table on the FG ONLY while
    // every band carries a live `dsb: 4`; a scan that paired dsa with deform_fg and dsb
    // with deform_bg would fold Haze to no-deform. The runtime's shift sentinel is per
    // band and per plane, not per attachment.
    comptime var live_amp = 0
    // THE ONE-DOT RULE (Task-1 spike). This three-step split — field, then index, then
    // field — is not indirection for its own sake and must not be collapsed to
    // `s.sc_layers[i].ly_dsa`: chained access past one dot NEVER resolves, and it does
    // not error, it silently yields nothing. Every `ls` / `l` split in this file
    // (scene_hdr, scene_band, derived_mask) is here for that reason.
    let ls = s.sc_layers
    let n  = s.sc_count
    for i in 0..n {
        let l = ls[i]
        if l.ly_dsa != 15 {
            live_amp = 1
        }
        if l.ly_dsb != 15 {
            live_amp = 1
        }
    }
    if anchored == 1 {
        if scene_anchor_dsa(s.sc_anchor) != 15 {
            live_amp = 1
        }
        if scene_anchor_dsb(s.sc_anchor) != 15 {
            live_amp = 1
        }
    }
    if has_table == 1 && live_amp == 1 {
        caps = caps | $0004
    }
    if scene_vdeform_is_none(s.sc_v_deform) == 0 {
        caps = caps | $0002
    }
    if anchored == 1 {
        caps = caps | $0008
    }
    // $0020 CAP_MULTI_DEFORM_TABLE — P3 Task 9. THE ONE THING THAT RAISES IT: a layer
    // carrying `deform: Own(..)`. Scene-level `Shared` does NOT, and design §2 says so
    // outright ("this is what WindyHaze/SkyHaze/haze_fg actually are; does NOT trip
    // MULTI_DEFORM_TABLE") — nor does layer-level `None`, which IS shared(phase). All
    // twenty shipped scenes are Shared/None, so this arm folds 0 across the registry and
    // games/sonic4/data/effects/scene_registry.emp pins that: the bit staying clear is
    // what keeps BAND_EXT_N at 0 and the four canonical images byte-identical.
    //
    // A VARIANT TEST, THROUGH derived_own() — the same walk scene()'s guard uses, so a
    // scene the guard approved cannot fold to a different shape than the one it checked.
    if derived_own(ls, n) == 1 {
        caps = caps | $0020
    }
    // $0040 CAP_FACTOR_CURVE — P3 Task 10. THE ONE THING THAT RAISES IT: a layer carrying
    // `curve: To(..)`. It is a property of the LAYER, never of the scene: there is no
    // scene-level curve slot, because a ramp is a statement about one layer's own factor.
    // All twenty shipped scenes are curve-free, so this arm folds 0 across the registry and
    // games/sonic4/data/effects/scene_registry.emp pins that — the bit staying clear is what
    // keeps BAND_CURVE_N at 0 and the four canonical images byte-identical.
    //
    // A VARIANT TEST, THROUGH derived_curve() — the same walk scene()'s anchor guard uses.
    if derived_curve(ls, n) == 1 {
        caps = caps | $0040
    }
    // MULTI_DEFORM_TABLE IMPLIES CAP_DEFORM, and the implication is STRUCTURAL, not
    // hopeful — which is what makes it worth pinning rather than asserting in a comment:
    // layer() refuses an own() whose two shifts are both 15, so an own() layer always
    // contributes live_amp, and it always contributes a table (scene() requires a
    // scene-level one as well). has_table && live_amp is $0004's rule. (Until 2026-08-26
    // this pin also asserted CAP_PER_LINE; that bit is retired.)
    // The RUNTIME depends on it: the per-band table reload in
    // Parallax_Fill_PerLine is emitted INSIDE the CAP_DEFORM gate (a5/a6 are the sampling
    // block's registers and nothing else reads them), so a mask carrying $0020 without
    // $0004 would elide the site the bit exists to select and tools/scene_spans.py would
    // demand a span from a build where the whole enclosing block was gone.
    ensure((caps & $0020) == 0 || (caps & $0004) != 0,
           "scene_caps(): a scene raised CAP_MULTI_DEFORM_TABLE ({caps}) without CAP_DEFORM. It is supposed to follow structurally — layer() refuses an own() table whose amplitude shifts are both 15 — so this firing means that guard was relaxed. The per-band table reload is emitted inside the CAP_DEFORM gate; a mask without it elides the site the bit selects")
    return caps
}

// fold_caps — the REGISTRY-level capability mask: every scene's demands, ORed, plus the
// one bit no single scene can raise.
//
// It lives here rather than in Task 5's registry module because it is the other half of
// scene_caps() and the two must be read together — split across files, the reason
// CAP_TRANSITIONS is missing upstairs stops being visible from where it is added. The
// registry module supplies the LIST; the rule for turning a list into a mask is model,
// and model lives with the model.
//
// CAP_TRANSITIONS ($0010) on count > 1: a transition is a property of the EDGE between
// two scenes, so a game with one scene never crosses one no matter what its sc_transition
// byte says. This is the P1 ruling as written; a later phase that learns which section
// pairs are actually adjacent can tighten it, and the tightening is a strict subset.
pub comptime fn fold_caps(scenes: array) -> int {
    ensure(scenes.len >= 1, "fold_caps(): an empty scene registry has no capabilities to fold — a game with no scenes should not be declaring a cap mask")
    comptime var caps = 0
    for s in scenes {
        caps = caps | scene_caps(s)
    }
    if scenes.len > 1 {
        caps = caps | $0010
    }
    return caps
}

// =====================================================================
// THE REBASE STRUCTURAL CHECK (design §6 / §8.2's last build-time gate).
//
// READ THE SCOPE FIRST. Design §6 says: "world-Y layer tops and the anchor bank join the
// §4.11 rebase checklist. Until §4.11 exists (it is FUTURE/Phase-4), the contract test is
// STRUCTURAL/DECLARATIVE only — an act-relative tagging check — stated plainly so it
// cannot be mistaken for runtime rebase verification." This is that check and nothing
// more. It proves the DECLARATIONS sit in a rebase-classified coordinate space; it does
// not, and cannot, prove a rebase preserves them.
//
// THE TWO SPACES, AND WHY EACH IS SAFE UNDER §4.11's UNIFORM SUBTRACTION:
//
//   layer top  (SceneLayer.ly_world_y)  ACT-RELATIVE SINCE P3 TASK 7, AND THAT CHANGED
//              ITS REBASE ARGUMENT — read this before assuming the old one still holds.
//              It USED to be plane-relative (a position inside the 512-px Plane-B span,
//              folded to a cell row by `scene_band`'s `>> 3`), and the argument was that
//              it carried no absolute act coordinate, so a uniform subtraction of
//              REBASE_DELTA left it correct untouched — the section-local-entity argument
//              (ARCH §4.9.1). THAT IS NO LONGER TRUE. `ly_world_y` is now an ACT Y, so a
//              rebase that renumbers act coordinates MUST renumber it, exactly as it must
//              renumber the anchor bank. Design §6 anticipated this ("world-Y layer tops
//              and the anchor bank join the §4.11 rebase checklist"); this comment is the
//              record that they have now actually joined it. The mitigating fact, and the
//              reason this is a checklist row rather than a blocker: the tops are
//              COMPTIME data folded to plane lines by `scene_plane_line()` at build time,
//              so a rebase is a re-authoring event, not a runtime one — nothing in RAM
//              holds a stale copy.
//
//   anchor     (SceneAnchor.At's first payload)  ACT-RELATIVE, BY INDIRECTION. The scene
//              declares a patch CHANNEL, never a coordinate; the act-space Y lives in
//              Effects_World_Y[ch] and reaches the overlay through Effects_Screen_L,
//              which Effects_LatchWorldLines re-derives every frame from Camera_Y. So the
//              rebase has exactly one bank to renumber and the scene records nothing that
//              could go stale.
//
// WHY IT IS A REGISTRY-LEVEL FOLD AND NOT A THIRD ENSURE IN scene()/layer(). Those two
// already range-check their own arguments — and if that were the whole story this check
// would be vacuous, which is worth saying out loud. It is not, because BOTH constructors
// are bypassable and this file says so twice: `SceneLayer{ ly_world_y: 5, .. }` and
// `Scene{ .. }` both type-check and skip every guard (see the notes at those structs).
// A value that reached the registry through that door has never been range-checked by
// anything. This fold runs over the REGISTRY — every scene the game actually ships,
// however it was built — which is the only place that door can be closed.
//
// Returns the number of world-Y tops it classified, so the caller can reference the
// result: an unreferenced top-level `const X = f(...)` is comptime-INERT and proves
// nothing (docs/EMP_PITFALLS.md §3).
// =====================================================================
pub comptime fn assert_act_relative_tagged(scenes: array, act_span: int) -> int {
    // ANTI-VACUITY ON THE BOUND ITSELF. A caller that passed 0 — or a name that resolved
    // to nothing, which is the silent failure mode for a constant at a call site
    // (docs/EMP_PITFALLS.md §2) — would make every top below fail rather than pass, so
    // this is a diagnosis rather than a net. A NEGATIVE or absurdly small span is the
    // case worth naming: it would make the fold assert something no act could satisfy.
    // 512 is inlined per the pin block; one Plane-B span is the smallest act that can
    // carry a scene at all.
    ensure(act_span >= 512 && act_span <= $8000,
           "scene registry: the act span handed to assert_act_relative_tagged() is {act_span}, which is not a legal act extent (one Plane-B span .. the engine's $8000 act-axis ceiling). This value must be DERIVED from the act descriptor's own grid — GRID_H << SECTION_SIZE_SHIFT — not typed; a 0 here usually means the constant did not resolve at the call site")
    comptime var tops = 0
    comptime var anchors = 0
    for s in scenes {
        // The one-dot rule again — field, then index, then field (see scene_caps()).
        let ls = s.sc_layers
        let n  = s.sc_count
        for i in 0..n {
            let l  = ls[i]
            let wy = l.ly_world_y
            // P3 TASK 7 CHANGED BOTH HALVES OF THIS ENSURE, AND THE SPACE IT NAMES.
            //   * the 8-px grid clause is GONE: an off-grid top is legal now, and the
            //     per-line fill (the only fill since 2026-08-26) places it exactly.
            //   * `wy < 512` — the BG PLANE's span — became `wy < act_span`, the act's
            //     own, because that is the space `ly_world_y` is now in. The number is
            //     DERIVED at the call site from the act descriptor, never typed here.
            // THE PLANE-IMAGE RANGE IS DELIBERATELY NOT RE-CHECKED HERE. `scene_plane_line()`
            // owns it, and every scene in this list reaches it: lowerN calls scene_band()
            // once per real layer and scene_band() calls scene_plane_line(). Adding a second
            // call here would be a guard that can never fire on its own — indistinguishable
            // from coverage, which is this tree's most expensive lesson. What THIS ensure
            // owns is the act span, which nothing else in the chain knows.
            ensure(wy >= 0 && wy < act_span,
                   "scene registry: a world-Y layer top ({wy}) is outside this act's vertical span ({act_span}), so it is not act-relative-tagged and the §4.11 rebase has no class for it. NOTE this is a STRUCTURAL check only: it proves the declaration is tagged, NOT that a rebase preserves it. Runtime rebase verification waits on the §4.11 mechanism (Phase 4); do not read a green build here as rebase-safe. A top that reached here out of span came through the SceneLayer{{ .. }} back door, bypassing layer()'s own guards")
            tops = tops + 1
        }
        // $FF = PARALLAX_ANCHOR_NONE, 4 = RASTER_MAX_PATCH — both inlined per the pin block.
        let ch = scene_anchor_ch(s.sc_anchor)
        ensure(ch == $FF || ch < 4,
               "scene registry: an anchor bank reference (channel {ch}) is not act-relative-tagged — the ACT-RELATIVE space is the patch bank Effects_World_Y[4], and a channel outside it names no bank the §4.11 rebase would renumber. NOTE this is a STRUCTURAL check only: it proves the declaration is tagged, NOT that a rebase preserves it. Runtime rebase verification waits on the §4.11 mechanism (Phase 4); do not read a green build here as rebase-safe. A channel that reached here out of range came through the Scene{{ .. }} back door, bypassing scene()'s own guards")
        if ch != $FF {
            anchors = anchors + 1
        }
    }
    // ANTI-VACUITY FLOOR. A registry whose scenes declare no world-Y top at all would
    // satisfy every ensure above by having nothing to check, and the caller's `> 0`
    // reference would still hold on the anchor count alone. Both are named so a green
    // result reports what it actually looked at.
    ensure(tops >= 1,
           "scene registry: not one world-Y layer top was classified, so the rebase structural check asked nothing. A scene registry with no tops is either empty or built through the Scene{{ .. }} back door with sc_count 0")
    return tops + anchors
}

// =====================================================================
// THE BUDGET LEDGER (P2 Task 11 — design §5).
//
// Every constant below is TRANSCRIBED FROM A MEASURED ROW in
// tools/effects_budget_model.toml, and each carries the row name it came from. Nothing
// here is estimated, and nothing here is copied from a nearby number: the plan's own
// preamble names `EFX_BLANK_DELAY = 4` as the cautionary case — a constant fitted to one
// shape, enforced by a guard, and wrong for every other shape for as long as nothing
// measured it. Phase 0 measured these; the derivation of each budget is written beside it.
//
// ---- WHY THE AGGREGATION IS `max`, NOT `sum` (a correction to the plan) ----
//
// Design §5 and the plan say "per axis, the SUM is enforced comptime". For SCENES that is
// wrong, and not marginally: ONLY ONE SCENE IS LIVE AT A TIME. A game with twenty scenes
// does not run twenty walkers — it runs the one its current section binds. Summing axis 1
// over the shipped twenty gives ~500,000 cycles against a 128,000-cycle frame, so a SUM
// gate would refuse a registry that demonstrably runs today, which is the exact failure the
// toml header warns about ("a generated `ensure` would fail programs that demonstrably run").
//
// So the steady-state fold is `max` — the WORST scene must fit — and that is the honest
// enforcement. The place a pairwise SUM genuinely belongs is the TRANSITION FRAME, where
// outgoing and incoming configs are both partially live; that is Task 12, and it is BLOCKED
// (no comptime section->scene join, no measured transition reservation — see
// docs/DEFERRED_WORK.md). This note is the seam: when Task 12 lands, it adds the pairwise
// term ON TOP of these max rows; it does not replace them.
//
// ---- WHY THE DENOMINATOR IS THE IDLE STATE ----
//
// Phase 0 measured that sustained max-diagonal does NOT run at 60 Hz: 31 video frames carry
// 15 logic ticks (frames_per_logic_tick_max_diagonal = 2.067, spread 0 across five boots),
// and the single biggest term is Tile_Cache_Fill at 40.1% of every video frame. That is an
// ART-STREAMING cost, not an effects cost, and it is separately owned. A budget gate that
// certifies "this scene fits a 60 Hz frame" must therefore divide by the state that ACTUALLY
// HOLDS 60 Hz, which is idle. The max-diagonal rows stay in the toml as context, carried
// with BOTH denominators, and are never a gate divisor.
//
// ---- UNITS: HUNDREDTHS OF A CYCLE ----
//
// The walker's fitted constants are fractional to two places (line_bg_only 26.90, and the
// anchor slope 60.77). `.emp` comptime is integer math, so the axis-1 terms are held x100
// and named `_X100`. Rounding to whole cycles instead would discard a measured digit for no
// reason; the raster DSL already sets this precedent ("Computed in TWENTIETHS so the two
// halvings ... are exact").
// =====================================================================

// ---- Axis 1: the walker's fitted additive model, [parallax.cost_model] ----
// 26 fixtures, 3 boots each, spread 0 on every one; residual 0.00 cyc on the un-anchored
// subset (18 fixtures). Response = Parallax_Update's per-routine row, INCLUSIVE of its
// callees. RE-TAKEN AND PROMOTED by P3 Task 13 (2026-08-22) over the walker P3 leaves
// behind — the post-unroll, world-Y-re-glue filler at the P3 tip (ROM crc 0dbaa80f);
// evidence WALKER-MODEL.md §11. The P2-era transcription that stood here until then was
// measured against the PRE-unroll filler and predated three fitted columns, all charged
// below now: shift_lines, band_sampling, and the anchor's fitted (base + per-trip) pair.
// BASE IS THE PER-LINE FLOOR SINCE 2026-08-26 (d-29-corrected), and it is a DERIVED SUM,
// not a re-measurement: the fitted model had `base` (W0 — one band on the per-cell
// filler, 3116.00) plus `line_mode` (W0->W4, +1548.00 for selecting the per-line filler
// and flipping reg $0B). The per-cell filler is deleted, so every scene pays both and the
// two only ever appear summed: 3116.00 + 1548.00 = 4664.00. `band_percell` (752.00) had
// no subject left and is gone. Every scene in the registry was already per-line, so no
// axis-1 charge moved. The next parallax_cost_probe re-take fits ONE base column
// (toml `base_cycles`) against the surviving fixtures and replaces this sum with a
// measurement; until then the toml row carries the same derivation.
pub const SB_WALK_BASE_X100          = 466400   // base_cycles          4664.00 = 3116.00 + 1548.00
pub const SB_WALK_BAND_PERLINE_X100  = 85400    // band_perline_cycles   854.00
pub const SB_WALK_MULTIBAND_X100     = 2000     // multiband_cycles        20.00
// THE PER-LINE SAMPLING TERMS ARE SHIFT-0 INTERCEPTS since the shift_lines column exists;
// the amplitude rides SB_WALK_SHIFT_LINE_X100 below. And they are POST-UNROLL values: the
// single-channel loops carry a ~154-cycle per-band prologue (SB_WALK_SAMP_BAND_X100) that
// bought the per-line body down from the pre-unroll 76.21/76.27; `.lp_both` is deliberately
// un-unrolled (register-file ruling, P3 C2), which is why a both-planes line costs ~4.8x a
// single-channel one rather than ~2x.
pub const SB_WALK_LINE_FG_X100       = 2600     // line_fg_only_cycles     26.00 (at shift 0)
pub const SB_WALK_LINE_BG_X100       = 2690     // line_bg_only_cycles     26.90 (at shift 0)
pub const SB_WALK_LINE_BOTH_X100     = 12453    // line_both_cycles       124.53 (at shift 0)
pub const SB_WALK_SHIFT_LINE_X100    = 200      // shift_lines_cycles       2.00 per line,
                                                // per unit of deform shift, per channel —
                                                // the `asr.w d3, d1` register-count shift
                                                // (6 + 2n), confirmed exactly by W22/W7/W23
pub const SB_WALK_SAMP_BAND_X100     = 15400    // band_sampling_cycles   154.00 per band
                                                // taking a sampling loop — the unrolled
                                                // loops' per-band prologue (W14/W15/W25).
                                                // A LOOP-SHAPE property: it read 0.00 on the
                                                // pre-unroll filler (WALKER-MODEL.md §5(d))
pub const SB_WALK_VDEFORM_X100       = 147200   // vdeform_cycles        1472.00

// THE CURVE'S TWO TERMS (P3 Task 10), and they are NOT from the fit above. Every other
// constant in this block came from the 26-fixture canonical sweep; these two were measured on
// a capability-raised instrument build, because no canonical image contains the mechanism —
// the same standing as the CAP_MULTI_DEFORM_TABLE row in the toml. `[parallax.cost_model]`
// `factor_curve_line_cycles` / `factor_curve_band_cycles`, curve-vs-flat pairs on one ROM.
//
// A CURVE LINE IS CHEAPER THAN A SAMPLED PRE-UNROLL LINE and dearer than an unrolled
// single-channel one (40.75 against line_both's 124.53 and line_fg_only's 26.00) and that
// is still the right shape: it reads no table, sign-extends nothing and shifts nothing — it
// adds a step, adds a remainder and takes one conditional correction, but it does so in the
// un-unrolled fill body. The band term is large by comparison because it carries a `divs.w`.
// NOT RE-TAKEN by Task 13: no canonical fixture can excite these (the capability is not
// raised in any shipped image), so the T10 instrument-build pairs remain the measurement.
pub const SB_WALK_LINE_CURVE_X100    = 4075     // factor_curve_line_cycles   40.75
pub const SB_WALK_BAND_CURVE_X100    = 63000    // factor_curve_band_cycles  630.00

// THE ANCHOR IS FITTED SINCE P3 TASK 1 AND CHARGED AT ITS COMPTIME-WORST TRIP COUNT. P2's
// "two regimes" (456.7 / 1204.7) were refuted — an instrument dilution plus the then-missing
// shift term (WALKER-MODEL.md §5(c)) — and the overlay now carries one fitted pair:
// anchor 981.4 + 60.77 x overlay_loop_trips, max |residual| 58.9 over 8 fixtures (the
// residual is the recorded, deliberately-unfitted loop-type effect). The trip count is a
// runtime quantity — it depends on the split band index k, which Effects_LatchWorldLines
// re-derives from Camera_Y every frame — so the budget charges the k that maximises it
// (k = 0: trips = 3n - 1 for n >= 2; see scene_anchor_worst_trips below). This
// over-charges a real camera position by design, same reasoning as SB_SCREEN_LINES.
pub const SB_WALK_ANCHOR_BASE_X100   = 98140    // anchor_cycles          981.40
pub const SB_WALK_ANCHOR_OP_X100     = 6077     // anchor_ops_cycles       60.77 per trip

// THE SAMPLING TERMS ARE PER SCREEN LINE — forced by measurement, not chosen: fixtures that
// sample only their lower band (112 and 80 lines) give the same per-line marginal as the
// whole-screen one, three independent times (WALKER-MODEL.md §5(b), re-confirmed on the
// post-unroll filler). THE LINE COUNT A BUDGET MUST USE IS 224, THE WHOLE SCREEN, even for an anchored
// scene that samples fewer. The anchor's SCREEN line is a RUNTIME value — the scene declares
// a patch CHANNEL, and Effects_LatchWorldLines re-derives the line from Camera_Y every frame
// — so the comptime-knowable worst case is an anchor at screen line 0. This over-charges the
// shipped anchored scene (which samples 144 lines at the idle baseline) by design; a budget
// that assumed the camera's convenient position would be gating a coincidence.
pub const SB_SCREEN_LINES            = 224

// The out-of-sample gap, carried as an EXPLICIT uncertainty term rather than tuned away.
// The live config at the idle baseline predicts 13542.7 and measures 13834 — gap +291.3,
// or +2.11%, which the toml attributes to the shipped config's own band tops and
// scroll-factor shifts (work the fixtures hold constant; the growth from 1.10% happened at
// T7, whose re-glue moved the shipped config's band geometry — WALKER-MODEL.md §11).
// Applied as +22/1000 — rounded UP from 21.1, the conservative direction.
pub const SB_WALK_UNCERTAINTY_PERMIL = 22       // out_of_sample_gap_pct 2.11, rounded up

// ---- Axis 1 pool and reservation ----
// Pool: the NTSC video frame. Reservation: the engine cost that is NOT the walker, so the
// walker's own measured row is subtracted out — charging a scene the walker AND leaving the
// baseline walker in the reservation would double-count the very thing being budgeted.
// Re-derived by P3 Task 13 from the 2026-08-22 [engine_reservation] re-take (the walker's
// idle cost moved, so the subtraction moved with it; the P2 derivation was
// (35125 - 19511) + 8280 = 23894):
//   idle_main_loop_cycles 29472 - idle_parallax_update_cycles 13492 = 15980  (non-walker loop)
//   + idle_vblank_cycles 8277                                               (the VInt bracket)
//   = 24257
pub const SB_FRAME_CYCLES            = 128000   // NTSC video frame (ntsc_scanline_cycles 488 x 262)
pub const SB_AXIS1_RESERVATION       = 24257    // measured, derived above

// ---- Axis 2: VBlank DMA bytes ----
// Pool 7524 B is the H40 NTSC VBlank DMA capacity. The reservation is the measured queue
// MINUS the per-line HScroll entry, because that entry belongs to the SCENE and is what this
// axis charges: whole-region scan 3056 B, less 896 B, = 2160 B of non-scene engine DMA.
//
// THE PER-LINE FORCER IS 896 B, ONE ENTRY — and the 1792 B that stood here from P2 until
// 2026-08-20 was a measurement artifact, not a correction to design §5. P3 Task 6 Step 1
// ruled it: `Process_DMA_Critical` resets only the slot CURSOR on drain
// (engine/system/dma_queue.emp:362) and never zeroes the entry bytes, so the whole-region
// queue scan that reported "two 448-word entries with the same command word" was reporting
// one live entry and one stale copy from an earlier frame at a different slot offset. Three
// independent tells, all reproduced: the tree has exactly ONE `Static_Hscroll_Line` enqueue
// site and it is an either/or reached once per VBlank; at the scan line all three slot cursors
// sat at their BASES, so the queue was EMPTY and every entry reported was residue; and zeroing
// the region then advancing exactly one frame yields ONE 896 B entry, five trials, no
// variation. §5's own 896 B / 12% was right.
//
// THE 896 B DID NOT VANISH — IT MOVED COLUMNS. The reservation went 1264 -> 2160 so the SUM
// this guard is judged on (896 + 2160 = 3056) is byte-for-byte what it was, and no budget was
// loosened on the strength of a re-attribution. 2160 is a CEILING (it still contains drain
// residue); the live per-frame queue is 984 B, of which 896 is the scene's, leaving 88 B — but
// that state has an EMPTY Important queue, so 88 would under-reserve for art streaming, whose
// 1152 B page transfer this scan only ever sees as residue. P3 Task 13 (2026-08-22) re-took
// every input on the tip and reproduced them all (five boots, spread 0), so the ceiling
// STANDS; a true derivation of "standing non-scene DMA" needs a per-frame UNION of live
// enqueues across a streaming-active window — an instrument this tree does not have, booked
// in docs/DEFERRED_WORK.md with that unlock condition.
pub const SB_DMA_POOL_BYTES          = 7524
pub const SB_AXIS2_RESERVATION       = 2160     // CEILING: whole-region 3056 B less the scene's 896
pub const SB_PERLINE_DMA_BYTES       = 896      // ONE 448-word entry (Task 6 Step 1 ruling)

// ---- Axis 3: VBlank CPU, and the COMBINED per-line cost §5 asks for ----
// Pool: the ~18,200-cycle NTSC VBlank window ([raster.schedule_build]'s own denominator).
// Reservation: idle_vblank_cycles 8280 — the VInt_Level BRACKET, which the toml names as
// "the number a VBlank axis is a fraction of", not Raster_VBlank's narrower row.
//
// THE SCENE'S AXIS-3 CHARGE IS THE DRAIN OF ITS OWN AXIS-2 BYTES, and this is the half that
// makes the per-line cost legible as one number. It is DERIVED from documented hardware
// rates, exactly as the toml derives dma_max_single_transfer_derived_cycles: H40 VBlank VRAM
// DMA moves 205 B/line and a scanline is 3420/7 = 488.57 cyc, so 896 B occupies
// 896 / 205 * 488.57 = 2135 cycles. (HALVED 2026-08-20 with SB_PERLINE_DMA_BYTES above: this
// row is derived from that one, so the Task 6 Step 1 ruling carries straight through.)
//
// THIS IS NOT DOUBLE-COUNTING THE 8280, and the reason is the instrument. Oracle's 68000
// core adds only `cyclesExecuted` to `_currentCycle` while bus/VDP/DMA stall accumulates in
// `additionalTime` and lands in `_currentTime` (M68000.cpp:1029-1031; the profiler ring
// stamps `_currentCycle`, M68000.h:95). So DMA occupancy NEVER reaches a measured cycle row
// — 8280 is blind to it by construction — and adding the derived term supplies exactly what
// the instrument cannot see. It is also why max_contiguous_dma_stall_cycles is recorded as
// UNMEASURABLE-ON-THIS-INSTRUMENT rather than as a number.
pub const SB_VBLANK_WINDOW_CYCLES    = 18200
pub const SB_AXIS3_RESERVATION       = 8280     // idle_vblank_cycles, the VInt_Level bracket
pub const SB_DMA_BYTES_PER_LINE      = 205      // H40 VBlank VRAM DMA rate (documented)
pub const SB_SCANLINE_CYC_X100       = 48857    // 3420/7 = 488.57 cyc

// ---- Axis 4b lives in engine/effects/raster_dsl.emp ----
// The HInt per-frame total is a property of the authored RASTER PROGRAM, not of a scene:
// scenes do not fire HInts. Charging it per-scene would need the section->preset->program
// join, which is Labels end to end — the same barrier that blocks Task 12. So axis 4b is
// enforced where the programs are built, inside `raster_program()`, over the fires it can
// actually see. Named here so a reader of the ledger is not left wondering where it went.

// ---- Axis 5: sprite occupancy — the left-column mask policy's SAT charge (P3 Task 14) ----
// The scene-side subject is P3 Task 12's `left_column_mask` policy: a SpriteMask declarer
// buys one full-height 8-px opaque column strip from the engine, and that strip is the ONLY
// sprite cost a scene can author today (CAP_FG_SPRITE_STRIPS stays RESERVED past P3, no
// lowering). Factor0Lock and Accept cost zero sprites by construction.
//
// THE AXIS GATES ON THE PER-LINE SPRITE COUNT — the toml's `axis5_binding_ceiling` ruling
// (`axis5_task12_resolution`): a stacked column strip costs exactly ONE sprite and one
// strip-width of pixels on any given scanline REGARDLESS of how many table entries build it,
// and the per-line ceiling is the one a clustered scene reaches first (Render_Sprites spends
// code on exactly that problem — the SCANLINE_SPRITE_LIMIT soft band — and none on running
// out of the 80). The pixel and table sub-ceilings are enforced too so the accepted 7-slot
// table cost stays a RULING and never silently grows.
//
// POOLS AND RESERVATIONS, transcribed from tools/effects_budget_model.toml
// [engine_reservation] (Task 4's measured SAT occupancy, 2026-08-20: ORACLE headless,
// SAT walked from the VRAM link chain, five boots per state, spread 0; pools are H40
// datasheet limits / MAX_VDP_SPRITES). Drift is gated twice: the toml [symbols] rows pin
// each transcription below to its toml row (tools/effects_budget_check.py, canonical
// build), and check_axis5_mask_pricing() in the same tool re-derives the mask geometry
// from the shipped engine constants (SPRITE_MASK_HEIGHT / SPRITE_MASK_SIZE in
// engine/objects/sprites.emp) — those constants are NOT named here on purpose: sprites.emp
// is not a COMPTIME_HELPERS module, so its names would resolve at the registry call site
// and break silently (docs/EMP_PITFALLS.md §2). Transcribe + pin, never import.
//
// THE RESERVATION IS AN UPPER BOUND ON HEADROOM (`axis5_caveat`): the idle rows come from
// GameState_OJZScroll, a near-empty scroll test (1 debug marker + 2 rings). A populated
// level grows every row. The budgets here are ceilings a mask policy must fit UNDER, not
// promises of free space.
pub const SB_AXIS5_LINE_SPRITE_POOL         = 20   // h40_sprites_per_line_limit (datasheet)
pub const SB_AXIS5_LINE_SPRITE_RESERVATION  = 2    // idle_sat_worst_line_sprites (measured, scanline 5)
pub const SB_AXIS5_LINE_PIXEL_POOL          = 320  // h40_sprite_pixels_per_line_limit (datasheet)
pub const SB_AXIS5_LINE_PIXEL_RESERVATION   = 32   // idle_sat_worst_line_pixels (measured)
pub const SB_AXIS5_TABLE_POOL               = 80   // sat_table_entries (= MAX_VDP_SPRITES, code-derived)
pub const SB_AXIS5_TABLE_RESERVATION        = 3    // idle_sat_slots_used (measured)
// The strip's price at the shipped mask geometry (InsertSpriteMasks: 8x32 sprites, coverage
// rounded UP to whole ones):
//   table slots  = ceil(SB_SCREEN_LINES / SPRITE_MASK_HEIGHT) = ceil(224/32) = 7
//                  (toml sat_mask_slots_full_height — the accepted-7 ruling, NOT the 1
//                  design §2 priced)
//   pixels/line  = one 8-px column (toml sat_mask_pixels_per_line)
//   sprites/line = 1 — GEOMETRIC, not a toml row: the strip is vertically STACKED
//                  non-overlapping 32-line sprites, so any scanline intersects exactly one
//                  of them whatever SPRITE_MASK_HEIGHT is. Derived, not transcribed, which
//                  is why it has no [symbols] pin.
pub const SB_AXIS5_MASK_TABLE_SLOTS         = 7    // sat_mask_slots_full_height
pub const SB_AXIS5_MASK_LINE_PIXELS         = 8    // sat_mask_pixels_per_line
pub const SB_AXIS5_MASK_LINE_SPRITES        = 1    // stacked strip: exactly one per line

// =====================================================================
// Per-scene axis costs. Each returns the charge for ONE scene; the registry folds them.
// =====================================================================

// Does this scene attach an H-deform table on EITHER plane? Plane-agnostic on purpose,
// matching scene_caps()'s has_table: the per-line pipeline is whole-screen, there is no
// half-per-line.
pub comptime fn scene_has_table(s: Scene) -> int {
    comptime var t = 0
    if scene_deform_is_none(s.sc_deform_fg) == 0 {
        t = 1
    }
    if scene_deform_is_none(s.sc_deform_bg) == 0 {
        t = 1
    }
    return t
}

// Live deform amplitude on plane A / plane B. TWO functions, one per plane, because the
// RUNTIME's shift sentinel is per band AND per plane — the sampling term charged below
// depends on how many PLANES sample, and a cross-plane "is anything live" answer cannot
// tell line_fg_only (76.21) from line_both (126.17).
//
// This is deliberately a NARROWER question than scene_caps()'s live_amp, which is
// cross-plane because it only has to answer "does this scene need the deform math at all"
// (and must fold ParallaxConfig_Haze — an FG-only table with live `dsb` — to CAP_DEFORM).
// Both readings are correct for their own question; see scene_caps()'s note. The budget
// needs the per-plane split because line_fg_only (26.00) and line_both (124.53) are
// different constants.
pub comptime fn scene_live_amp_a(s: Scene) -> int {
    comptime var live = 0
    // The one-dot rule — field, then index, then field (see scene_caps()).
    let ls = s.sc_layers
    let n  = s.sc_count
    for i in 0..n {
        let l = ls[i]
        if l.ly_dsa != 15 {
            live = 1
        }
    }
    if scene_anchor_ch(s.sc_anchor) != $FF {
        if scene_anchor_dsa(s.sc_anchor) != 15 {
            live = 1
        }
    }
    return live
}
pub comptime fn scene_live_amp_b(s: Scene) -> int {
    comptime var live = 0
    let ls = s.sc_layers
    let n  = s.sc_count
    for i in 0..n {
        let l = ls[i]
        if l.ly_dsb != 15 {
            live = 1
        }
    }
    if scene_anchor_ch(s.sc_anchor) != $FF {
        if scene_anchor_dsb(s.sc_anchor) != 15 {
            live = 1
        }
    }
    return live
}

// The per-screen-line sampling term, x100. Zero when the scene is flat-pathed (a table
// attached but every consumer at shift 15 — the sample loop is skipped and the emitted
// HScroll is identical to the no-deform case; OJZ_Default is exactly that scene).
//
// A FLAT ACCUMULATOR OVER STATEMENT IFS, never nested if-expressions: an `if` in block-tail
// position evaluates to unit `()` SILENTLY in this language, and it has already folded a
// capability mask to 0 once in this very file's scene_caps() (docs/EMP_PITFALLS.md §1).
pub comptime fn scene_sample_term_x100(s: Scene) -> int {
    comptime var term = 0
    if scene_has_table(s) == 1 {
        let a = scene_live_amp_a(s)
        let b = scene_live_amp_b(s)
        if a == 1 {
            term = SB_WALK_LINE_FG_X100
        }
        if b == 1 {
            term = SB_WALK_LINE_BG_X100
        }
        // BOTH planes sampling gets its OWN measured constant (124.53, fixture W8), not
        // fg + bg. On the pre-unroll filler that was because both was CHEAPER than the sum
        // (shared index/phase work); on the post-unroll filler it is because both is
        // DEARER — `.lp_both` is deliberately un-unrolled (the register-file ruling, P3
        // spec-correction C2) while the single-channel loops were unrolled to ~26/line.
        // Either way, summing the single-channel constants would mis-book every two-plane
        // scene; the measured third column is the honest charge.
        if a == 1 {
            if b == 1 {
                term = SB_WALK_LINE_BOTH_X100
            }
        }
    }
    return term
}

// The worst-case deform SHIFT a plane's sampled lines can pay, per plane. The shift is the
// `asr.w d3, d1` register count in the sampled loops — 2 cycles per unit per line per
// channel (SB_WALK_SHIFT_LINE_X100) — and it is an AUTHORED field (ly_dsa/ly_dsb, plus the
// anchor's override pair), so the comptime-knowable worst case is the largest LIVE shift
// the scene declares on that plane, charged over the whole screen. Which lines actually
// sample at which band's shift is a runtime question (Step 4a rebases tops per frame), so
// per-band precision here would be gating a coincidence — same reasoning as
// SB_SCREEN_LINES' banner. 15 is the OFF sentinel, never a shift.
//
// THE CLAMP MIRRORS scene_curve_bands()'s, for the same measured reason: these folds are
// reached from the axis-1 term for EVERY scene, including poison_budget_axis1's back-door
// fixtures whose sc_count (115/116) exceeds the 16-element layer array.
pub comptime fn scene_max_shift_a(s: Scene) -> int {
    comptime var m = 0
    let ls  = s.sc_layers
    let cnt = if s.sc_count > 16 { 16 } else { s.sc_count }
    for i in 0..cnt {
        let l = ls[i]
        if l.ly_dsa != 15 {
            if l.ly_dsa > m {
                m = l.ly_dsa
            }
        }
    }
    if scene_anchor_ch(s.sc_anchor) != $FF {
        if scene_anchor_dsa(s.sc_anchor) != 15 {
            if scene_anchor_dsa(s.sc_anchor) > m {
                m = scene_anchor_dsa(s.sc_anchor)
            }
        }
    }
    return m
}
pub comptime fn scene_max_shift_b(s: Scene) -> int {
    comptime var m = 0
    let ls  = s.sc_layers
    let cnt = if s.sc_count > 16 { 16 } else { s.sc_count }
    for i in 0..cnt {
        let l = ls[i]
        if l.ly_dsb != 15 {
            if l.ly_dsb > m {
                m = l.ly_dsb
            }
        }
    }
    if scene_anchor_ch(s.sc_anchor) != $FF {
        if scene_anchor_dsb(s.sc_anchor) != 15 {
            if scene_anchor_dsb(s.sc_anchor) > m {
                m = scene_anchor_dsb(s.sc_anchor)
            }
        }
    }
    return m
}

// The worst-case Step-4b overlay trip count for an n-band anchored scene. The overlay's
// four loops run min(k+1, n-1) + 2 x (n-1-k) + (n-k) iterations total, where k — the band
// the split lands in — is a RUNTIME quantity (Effects_LatchWorldLines re-derives the split
// line from Camera_Y every frame). For k <= n-2 the total is 3n - 1 - 2k, maximised at
// k = 0; at k = n-1 it is n, always smaller for n >= 2. A single-band anchored scene runs
// exactly 1 (find_k never iterates, no shifts, one override write).
pub comptime fn scene_anchor_worst_trips(n: int) -> int {
    comptime var t = 1
    if n >= 2 {
        t = 3 * n - 1
    }
    return t
}

// How many layers ramp their BG factor (P3 Task 10). The COUNT, not the boolean
// derived_curve() answers, because the per-band term is charged per curve band — the hoist
// decodes a factor and runs a divide for each one.
pub comptime fn scene_curve_bands(s: Scene) -> int {
    comptime var n = 0
    // The one-dot rule — field, then index, then field (see scene_caps()).
    let ls = s.sc_layers
    // CLAMPED TO THE ARRAY, and the clamp is not defensive programming — it is required, and
    // it was MEASURED. `scene()` ensures count <= 16, but `Scene{ .. }` type-checks and skips
    // every guard (see that struct's banner), and games/sonic4/test/poison/poison_budget_axis1
    // uses exactly that door to declare a 116-layer scene so the axis-1 ensure fires. Every
    // OTHER fold in this file survives that fixture only by accident: `scene_live_amp_a/b`
    // are reached through `if scene_has_table(s) == 1`, which that scene fails. This one is
    // called unconditionally from the axis-1 term, so it meets the back-door count head on —
    // 216 `[index.out-of-bounds]` diagnostics, measured 2026-08-20 when it was not clamped.
    let cnt = if s.sc_count > 16 { 16 } else { s.sc_count }
    for i in 0..cnt {
        let l = ls[i]
        if scene_curve_is_none(l.ly_curve) == 0 {
            n = n + 1
        }
    }
    return n
}

// AXIS 1 — the scene's main-loop cost in hundredths of a cycle, uncertainty included.
pub comptime fn scene_axis1_cycles_x100(s: Scene) -> int {
    let n        = s.sc_count
    // One filler, one floor, one per-band slope (2026-08-26): the per-cell mode and its
    // own slope are gone, and SB_WALK_BASE_X100 already carries the per-line selection.
    comptime var c = SB_WALK_BASE_X100
    c = c + SB_WALK_BAND_PERLINE_X100 * (n - 1)
    // ONCE, at bands >= 2, in BOTH modes — Step 4a's `.find_k` probe loop, whose body never
    // runs at band_count 1.
    if n >= 2 {
        c = c + SB_WALK_MULTIBAND_X100
    }
    let sample_term = scene_sample_term_x100(s)
    c = c + sample_term * SB_SCREEN_LINES
    if sample_term != 0 {
        // THE THREE COLUMNS THE P2-ERA TRANSCRIPTION PREDATED (P3 Task 13; the fitted model
        // has carried them since Task 1 and the fill-unroll made two of them large):
        //
        // (1) shift_lines — the sampled loops' `asr.w d3, d1` amplitude cost, 2.00 per line
        //     per shift unit PER CHANNEL, charged at each plane's worst authored shift over
        //     the whole screen (the per-plane fold documents why worst).
        comptime var shift_units = 0
        if scene_live_amp_a(s) == 1 {
            shift_units = shift_units + scene_max_shift_a(s)
        }
        if scene_live_amp_b(s) == 1 {
            shift_units = shift_units + scene_max_shift_b(s)
        }
        c = c + SB_WALK_SHIFT_LINE_X100 * shift_units * SB_SCREEN_LINES
        // (2) band_sampling — the unrolled loops' ~154-cycle per-band prologue. Which bands
        //     take a sampling loop is runtime (the anchor's overlay can turn a band's lower
        //     SEGMENT on, making n+1 walkable segments out of n bands), so the worst case
        //     is every segment sampling: n, plus one for an anchored split.
        comptime var samp_bands = n
        if scene_anchor_ch(s.sc_anchor) != $FF {
            samp_bands = samp_bands + 1
        }
        c = c + SB_WALK_SAMP_BAND_X100 * samp_bands
    }
    // CURVES (P3 Task 10), charged at the SAME worst case the sampling term uses: the whole
    // screen. A layer's on-screen line span is a RUNTIME quantity — Step 4a rebases the tops
    // against this frame's Vscroll_BG and clamps them, so a layer authored 64 px tall can
    // own anything from 0 to 224 screen lines — and the comptime-knowable worst case is one
    // curve layer covering the screen. This over-charges a small curve layer by design, for
    // the reason SB_SCREEN_LINES' own banner gives: a budget that assumed a convenient camera
    // would be gating a coincidence.
    //
    // ADDED TO THE SAMPLING TERM, NOT MAXED WITH IT. A layer may not have both (layer()
    // refuses the pair), but a SCENE may carry a curve layer and a sampled layer, and the
    // screen is then shared between them. Summing both at 224 lines charges each for the
    // whole screen — conservative, and the conservative direction.
    let curve_bands = scene_curve_bands(s)
    if curve_bands > 0 {
        // TWO STATEMENTS, NOT ONE EXPRESSION SPLIT OVER TWO LINES: .emp has no line
        // continuation, and a trailing `+` on the next line is `expected an expression,
        // found Plus` at a line number that is not the one you edited.
        c = c + SB_WALK_LINE_CURVE_X100 * SB_SCREEN_LINES
        c = c + SB_WALK_BAND_CURVE_X100 * curve_bands
    }
    if scene_vdeform_is_none(s.sc_v_deform) == 0 {
        c = c + SB_WALK_VDEFORM_X100
    }
    // The anchored overlay, FITTED (base + per-trip) since P3 Task 1 and charged at the
    // comptime-worst trip count — see SB_WALK_ANCHOR_BASE_X100's banner. This replaces the
    // flat 1204.7 "worst measured regime" charge, which came from a model with no anchor
    // column at all and belonged to the pre-unroll walker.
    if scene_anchor_ch(s.sc_anchor) != $FF {
        c = c + SB_WALK_ANCHOR_BASE_X100
        c = c + SB_WALK_ANCHOR_OP_X100 * scene_anchor_worst_trips(n)
    }
    // The measured out-of-sample gap, added rather than absorbed.
    return c + c * SB_WALK_UNCERTAINTY_PERMIL / 1000
}

// AXIS 2 — the scene's VBlank DMA bytes: the one 896-byte HScroll entry, for every scene
// (the 112-byte per-cell alternative was deleted 2026-08-26; the `s` parameter stays so
// the axis has the same shape as the others).
pub comptime fn scene_axis2_dma_bytes(s: Scene) -> int {
    return SB_PERLINE_DMA_BYTES + 0 * s.sc_count
}

// AXIS 3 — the scene's VBlank CPU: the drain occupancy of its own axis-2 bytes.
pub comptime fn scene_axis3_vblank_cycles(s: Scene) -> int {
    let bytes = scene_axis2_dma_bytes(s)
    // bytes / 205 lines, each 488.57 cyc — the x100 scanline constant divided back out.
    return bytes * SB_SCANLINE_CYC_X100 / (SB_DMA_BYTES_PER_LINE * 100)
}

// AXIS 5 — the scene's sprite charge, on each of the three SAT sub-ceilings. The ONLY
// policy that costs sprites is SpriteMask (see the SB_AXIS5_* banner); Factor0Lock,
// Accept and Undeclared all price to zero. scene() refuses an authored SpriteMask until
// its engine emission lands, so today every fold over these is zero — but the pricing is
// written for the future adopter world on purpose: the day the emission lands and a scene
// declares SpriteMask, that scene is charged here with no further wiring, and a Scene{ .. }
// back-door construction carrying SpriteMask is charged already.
pub comptime fn scene_axis5_line_sprites(s: Scene) -> int {
    return scene_lcm_is_sprite_mask(s.sc_left_col_mask) * SB_AXIS5_MASK_LINE_SPRITES
}
pub comptime fn scene_axis5_line_pixels(s: Scene) -> int {
    return scene_lcm_is_sprite_mask(s.sc_left_col_mask) * SB_AXIS5_MASK_LINE_PIXELS
}
pub comptime fn scene_axis5_table_slots(s: Scene) -> int {
    return scene_lcm_is_sprite_mask(s.sc_left_col_mask) * SB_AXIS5_MASK_TABLE_SLOTS
}

// =====================================================================
// The registry-level fold and the ENFORCEMENT. This is the single authority: design §5's
// ownership rule puts enforcement in the comptime DSL, and the toml `[symbols]` rows pin
// PROVENANCE (drift detection) and never enforcement.
//
// Returns the number of scenes it checked, so the caller can reference the result — an
// unreferenced top-level `const X = f(...)` is comptime-INERT and proves nothing
// (docs/EMP_PITFALLS.md §3), the same reason assert_act_relative_tagged() returns a count.
// =====================================================================
pub comptime fn scene_budget_enforce(scenes: array) -> int {
    ensure(scenes.len >= 1, "scene_budget_enforce(): an empty scene registry has no budget to check — a game with no scenes should not be reaching the budget fold")
    comptime var checked = 0
    for s in scenes {
        // AXIS 1 — main-loop cycles.
        let a1 = scene_axis1_cycles_x100(s)
        ensure(a1 / 100 + SB_AXIS1_RESERVATION <= SB_FRAME_CYCLES,
               "scene budget AXIS 1 (main-loop cycles): this scene's walker costs {a1 / 100} cyc against a {SB_FRAME_CYCLES}-cycle NTSC frame with {SB_AXIS1_RESERVATION} cyc reserved for the non-walker engine (idle_main_loop 29472 less the walker's own idle_parallax_update 13492, plus the idle_vblank VInt bracket 8277 — the 2026-08-22 P3 Task 13 re-take), leaving {SB_FRAME_CYCLES - SB_AXIS1_RESERVATION}. The cost is the FITTED walker model in tools/effects_budget_model.toml [parallax.cost_model] (26 fixtures, un-anchored residual 0.00 cyc, post-unroll + re-glue walker) plus its measured +2.11% out-of-sample gap, with the anchor charged as its fitted pair (981.4 + 60.77/trip) at the comptime-worst trip count 3n-1. Denominator is the IDLE camera state: sustained max-diagonal does not run at 60 Hz (1.192 video frames per logic tick post-streaming-arc) and is streaming-bound, so it is context, not a divisor")
        // AXIS 2 — VBlank DMA bytes.
        let a2 = scene_axis2_dma_bytes(s)
        ensure(a2 + SB_AXIS2_RESERVATION <= SB_DMA_POOL_BYTES,
               "scene budget AXIS 2 (VBlank DMA bytes): this scene enqueues {a2} B against a {SB_DMA_POOL_BYTES} B H40 NTSC VBlank pool with {SB_AXIS2_RESERVATION} B reserved for non-scene engine DMA (whole-region queue scan 3056 B less the scene's own per-line HScroll 896 B — a CEILING, since that scan includes drain residue; P3 Task 13 re-derives it), leaving {SB_DMA_POOL_BYTES - SB_AXIS2_RESERVATION}. A per-line scene costs 896 B — ONE 448-word HScroll entry, exactly what design §5 prices; the 1792 B this message carried until 2026-08-20 was a queue scan counting drain residue as a second live entry. Read it together with AXIS 3 below: the combined per-line tax is 896 B AND {scene_axis3_vblank_cycles(s)} cyc of drain, and the drain half is still the part §5 does not price at all")
        // AXIS 3 — VBlank CPU (the drain of axis 2).
        let a3 = scene_axis3_vblank_cycles(s)
        ensure(a3 + SB_AXIS3_RESERVATION <= SB_VBLANK_WINDOW_CYCLES,
               "scene budget AXIS 3 (VBlank CPU): this scene's DMA drain occupies {a3} cyc against the ~{SB_VBLANK_WINDOW_CYCLES}-cycle NTSC VBlank window with {SB_AXIS3_RESERVATION} cyc reserved (idle_vblank_cycles, the VInt_Level bracket Raster_VBlank runs inside), leaving {SB_VBLANK_WINDOW_CYCLES - SB_AXIS3_RESERVATION}. The drain is DERIVED from documented hardware rates (bytes / 205 B-per-line x 488.57 cyc-per-line), not measured, and it is NOT double-counted against the reservation: oracle's cycle clock excludes bus/DMA stall by construction (M68000.cpp:1029-1031), so no measured cycle row contains it")
        // AXIS 5 — sprite occupancy (P3 Task 14), one ensure per SAT sub-ceiling so an
        // overrun names the ceiling it crossed. The charge is per SCENE, never a sum over
        // the registry — one scene is live at a time (the P2 max ratification; the pairwise
        // sum belongs to the transition frame, P2 Task 12, still blocked on the Label join).
        // Today every scene prices to zero (SpriteMask has zero adopters and scene()
        // refuses it until the emission lands), and [scene_budget]'s falsifiability section
        // records this axis as NOT falsifiable with its unlock conditions — these ensures
        // are the ledger's derivation plus a backstop on the documented Scene{ .. } back
        // door, the same live role as axis 1's.
        let a5s = scene_axis5_line_sprites(s)
        ensure(a5s + SB_AXIS5_LINE_SPRITE_RESERVATION <= SB_AXIS5_LINE_SPRITE_POOL,
               "scene budget AXIS 5 (per-line sprites, the BINDING ceiling): this scene's left-column mask policy costs {a5s} sprite(s) on a worst-case scanline against the H40 hardware limit of {SB_AXIS5_LINE_SPRITE_POOL}/line with {SB_AXIS5_LINE_SPRITE_RESERVATION} reserved (idle_sat_worst_line_sprites, the Task-4 measured object-system floor — an UPPER BOUND on headroom, taken on a near-empty scroll test), leaving {SB_AXIS5_LINE_SPRITE_POOL - SB_AXIS5_LINE_SPRITE_RESERVATION}. A stacked column strip costs exactly 1 sprite on any line regardless of how many SAT entries build it (axis5_binding_ceiling, tools/effects_budget_model.toml)")
        let a5p = scene_axis5_line_pixels(s)
        ensure(a5p + SB_AXIS5_LINE_PIXEL_RESERVATION <= SB_AXIS5_LINE_PIXEL_POOL,
               "scene budget AXIS 5 (per-line sprite pixels): this scene's left-column mask policy costs {a5p} px on a worst-case scanline against the H40 hardware limit of {SB_AXIS5_LINE_PIXEL_POOL} px/line with {SB_AXIS5_LINE_PIXEL_RESERVATION} px reserved (idle_sat_worst_line_pixels, Task-4 measured), leaving {SB_AXIS5_LINE_PIXEL_POOL - SB_AXIS5_LINE_PIXEL_RESERVATION}. The strip is one 8-px column (sat_mask_pixels_per_line)")
        let a5t = scene_axis5_table_slots(s)
        ensure(a5t + SB_AXIS5_TABLE_RESERVATION <= SB_AXIS5_TABLE_POOL,
               "scene budget AXIS 5 (SAT table slots): this scene's left-column mask policy costs {a5t} of the {SB_AXIS5_TABLE_POOL} sprite-table entries with {SB_AXIS5_TABLE_RESERVATION} reserved (idle_sat_slots_used, Task-4 measured), leaving {SB_AXIS5_TABLE_POOL - SB_AXIS5_TABLE_RESERVATION}. A full-height strip at the shipped mask geometry is ceil(224/32) = 7 entries — the ACCEPTED-7 ruling (axis5_task12_resolution), priced openly rather than as the 1 design §2 assumed; this ensure is what keeps 7 a ruling instead of a drift")
        checked = checked + 1
    }
    return checked
}

// The worst scene per axis — the ledger rows the registry publishes as `pub equ`. `max`,
// not `sum`: see the banner. Each takes the registry so the answer is over every shipped
// scene, not over whichever one a reader happened to look at.
pub comptime fn scene_budget_max_axis1_x100(scenes: array) -> int {
    comptime var m = 0
    for s in scenes {
        let c = scene_axis1_cycles_x100(s)
        if c > m {
            m = c
        }
    }
    return m
}
pub comptime fn scene_budget_max_axis2(scenes: array) -> int {
    comptime var m = 0
    for s in scenes {
        let c = scene_axis2_dma_bytes(s)
        if c > m {
            m = c
        }
    }
    return m
}
pub comptime fn scene_budget_max_axis3(scenes: array) -> int {
    comptime var m = 0
    for s in scenes {
        let c = scene_axis3_vblank_cycles(s)
        if c > m {
            m = c
        }
    }
    return m
}
// Axis 5's three sub-ceiling folds (P3 Task 14). `max`, not `sum`, for the same reason as
// every fold here — and today each is 0 by construction (zero SpriteMask adopters, and
// scene() refuses the variant until its engine emission lands). A 0 in the ledger is the
// honest reading: it is the registry's worst sprite charge, not a placeholder.
pub comptime fn scene_budget_max_axis5_line_sprites(scenes: array) -> int {
    comptime var m = 0
    for s in scenes {
        let c = scene_axis5_line_sprites(s)
        if c > m {
            m = c
        }
    }
    return m
}
pub comptime fn scene_budget_max_axis5_line_pixels(scenes: array) -> int {
    comptime var m = 0
    for s in scenes {
        let c = scene_axis5_line_pixels(s)
        if c > m {
            m = c
        }
    }
    return m
}
pub comptime fn scene_budget_max_axis5_table_slots(scenes: array) -> int {
    comptime var m = 0
    for s in scenes {
        let c = scene_axis5_table_slots(s)
        if c > m {
            m = c
        }
    }
    return m
}

// =====================================================================
// Lowering — the authored scene becomes the records the runtime already reads.
//
// scene_hdr()/scene_band() must produce, FIELD FOR FIELD, what configs.emp's
// hdr()/band() produce for the same authoring. That is the P1 gate: Task 7 keeps the
// two constructors alive side by side and proves them word-equal, and Task 6's twenty
// migrated configs are only correct if the ROM does not move a byte. Every default
// below is hdr()'s default, transcribed, not re-derived.
// =====================================================================

// scene_hdr — the 30-byte parallax_config header (28 until the 2026-08-27 mask widening).
pub comptime fn scene_hdr(s: Scene) -> parallax_config {
    let n        = s.sc_count
    let mask_raw = s.sc_mask_raw
    let ls       = s.sc_layers
    // The layer mask: the raw bridge when authored, else the derived one. scene() has
    // already checked that a raw mask is a SUPERSET of this exact derivation, which is
    // why both call sites must share derived_mask() rather than each carrying a loop.
    let mask = if mask_raw >= 0 { mask_raw } else { derived_mask(ls, n) }
    // The V-deform amplitude shift: the raw bridge when authored (>= 0 wins outright,
    // including over an attached table — SkyHaze is the only case and attaches none),
    // else the attachment's own shift, else hdr()'s default 4 via the accessor.
    let vds_raw = s.sc_v_deform_shift_raw
    let vds = if vds_raw >= 0 { vds_raw } else { scene_vdeform_shift(s.sc_v_deform) }
    // pcfg_v_offset is the u16 IMAGE of a signed word (see Scene.sc_v_offset and scene()'s
    // v_offset guard): the runtime consumes it with add.w / move.w, so two's complement is
    // the encoding, and scene() has already bounded the value to the word, which makes this
    // single add total. A statement `if`, not an if-expression (docs/EMP_PITFALLS.md §1).
    comptime var vo_word = s.sc_v_offset
    if vo_word < 0 {
        vo_word = vo_word + $10000
    }
    return parallax_config{
        pcfg_band_count:        n,
        pcfg_v_factor_bg:       s.sc_v_factor,
        pcfg_layer_mask:        mask,
        pcfg_v_center_y:        s.sc_v_center,
        pcfg_v_offset:          vo_word,
        pcfg_transition:        s.sc_transition,
        pcfg_deform_speed_fg:   scene_deform_speed(s.sc_deform_fg),
        pcfg_deform_speed_bg:   scene_deform_speed(s.sc_deform_bg),
        pcfg_anchor_ch:         scene_anchor_ch(s.sc_anchor),
        pcfg_deform_table_fg:   scene_deform_table(s.sc_deform_fg),
        pcfg_deform_table_bg:   scene_deform_table(s.sc_deform_bg),
        pcfg_v_deform_table_bg: scene_vdeform_table(s.sc_v_deform),
        pcfg_v_deform_speed_bg: scene_vdeform_speed(s.sc_v_deform),
        pcfg_v_deform_shift_bg: vds,
        pcfg_anchor_dsa:        scene_anchor_dsa(s.sc_anchor),
        pcfg_anchor_dsb:        scene_anchor_dsb(s.sc_anchor),
        pcfg_v_factor_fg:       s.sc_v_factor_fg,
        // Spelt, not defaulted — the field carries no `= 0` in engine/structs.emp
        // deliberately, so that a third constructor cannot forget the even-size pad byte
        // and so that tools/left_col_mask_probe.py's field parser (which skips any field
        // with a default) keeps seeing the real sizeof.
        pcfg_pad_29:            0,
    }
}

// =====================================================================
// scene_plane_line — THE ACT -> PLANE MAPPING (P3 Task 7). The one unit conversion left
// in the lowering, and it replaced `world_y >> 3`, which was an act->cell TRUNCATION
// dressed up as a unit conversion.
//
// A layer top is authored in ACT world pixels. The band it becomes lives in the BG
// PLANE, and the plane does not track the act 1:1 — it tracks it through the scene's own
// vertical mapping, the SAME one Parallax_Step5_Vscroll applies to the camera:
//
//     Vscroll_BG = ((camY - v_center) >> v_factor) + v_offset      (parallax.emp:1062-1067)
//
// So the plane line an authored world Y lands on is that expression evaluated at the TOP
// instead of at the camera, and Step 4a's whole per-frame job is the subtraction of the
// two. Folding it at comptime rather than at runtime is deliberate: v_factor/v_center/
// v_offset are scene constants, so a runtime mapping would recompute an invariant up to
// MAX_PARALLAX_BANDS times a frame to reach the same number.
//
// WHY THIS IS A DIFFERENT MAPPING FROM THE ANCHOR'S, WHICH IS ALSO ACT-SPACE AND ALSO
// LANDS ON A SCREEN LINE. `Effects_LatchWorldLines` maps `Effects_World_Y[ch]` to a
// screen line as `world_y - Camera_Y`, 1:1, because a patch anchor is a LEVEL feature —
// a water surface Sonic swims under, at the camera's own depth. A static layer top is a
// feature of the BG ART, which is at the BG plane's depth. Two things authored in the
// same coordinate space land on different screen lines, and that is not an inconsistency,
// it is parallax. The two agree at exactly one depth (v_factor 0, v_center 0, v_offset 0)
// and are meant to disagree everywhere else.
//
// THE LOCKED CASE IS NOT A SPECIAL CASE, IT IS THE DEGENERATE ONE. `v_factor == 15` is
// the lock sentinel (parallax.emp:1055-1057): the BG plane ignores the camera entirely
// and Vscroll_BG is pinned at v_offset. There is no act->plane relation to invert —
// `(wy - v_center) >> 15` is 0 or -1 for every wy, which would collapse every layer of
// every locked scene onto one line. For a locked plane the authoring space IS the plane,
// so the mapping is the identity. EIGHTEEN OF THE TWENTY shipped scenes are that case
// (tops 0/32/80/112/160, which read as screen lines because v_offset is 0), which is why
// this arm carries the shipped registry rather than being a corner.
// =====================================================================
pub comptime fn scene_plane_line(s: Scene, wy: int) -> int {
    let vf = s.sc_v_factor
    let vc = s.sc_v_center
    let vo = s.sc_v_offset
    // A locked plane maps identically; an unlocked one may not shift a NEGATIVE dividend
    // (the runtime's `asr.w` sign-extends, so the top would land above the plane origin,
    // which is not a position). 15 is the lock sentinel, inlined per the pin block.
    ensure(vf == 15 || wy >= vc,
           "scene_plane_line(): world_y {wy} is above this scene's v_center {vc}. An unlocked BG plane maps act Y to a plane line as ((world_y - v_center) >> v_factor) + v_offset — the same mapping Parallax_Step5_Vscroll applies to the camera — and a top above v_center has no plane image, because the plane never reaches it. Move the top down, or move v_center up.")
    comptime var pl = wy
    if vf != 15 {
        pl = ((wy - vc) >> vf) + vo
    }
    // 512 is inlined per the pin block (PLANE_B_SPAN). Step 4a's runtime clamp protects
    // the HScroll buffer from an out-of-range top; this ensure is what stops a scene ever
    // needing it.
    ensure(pl >= 0 && pl < 512,
           "scene_plane_line(): world_y {wy} maps to plane line {pl}, outside the 512-px Plane-B span. Step 4a rotates tops modulo that span, so a top outside it aliases onto another band's rows instead of being clipped.")
    return pl
}

// =====================================================================
// scene_vsplit_line — a vertical split's SCREEN line (P3 Task 11).
//
// THREE SPACES, AND THIS FN IS THE SECOND HOP OF TWO. `scene_plane_line()` above maps the
// authored ACT Y to a PLANE line; Step 4a maps a plane line to a SCREEN line every frame as
// `plane_line - (Vscroll_BG mod 512)`. A raster fire needs the third space at COMPTIME, and
// the only scenes where that is possible are the ones the two-writer ruling already leaves:
// on a LOCKED plane `Vscroll_BG` is pinned at `v_offset` (parallax.emp's `.v_locked` arm —
// "locked: BG = vOffset (static, ignores camera + lerp)"), a scene constant. So
//
//     screen = scene_plane_line(s, wy) - v_offset          (v_factor == 15)
//
// and for the eighteen shipped locked scenes, whose v_offset is 0 and whose plane mapping is
// the identity, that is simply the authored top — which is why their tops (0/32/80/112/160)
// have always read as screen lines.
//
// THE LOCK TEST IS REPEATED HERE ON PURPOSE and is not a duplicate of scene()'s. scene()
// guards the AUTHORED path; `Scene{ .. }` type-checks and skips it (see the struct's note),
// and this fn is where a back-door scene would otherwise get a screen line computed from an
// expression that is only valid under the lock. A guard that can fire on input the other one
// never sees is not a second copy.
//
// THE SCREEN-LINE RANGE IS DELIBERATELY NOT CHECKED HERE. `fire()` owns 3..223 and says why
// (lines 0-2 are the priming records'), every fire this lowering builds goes through it, and
// a second range guard here could never fire on its own — the shape this tree keeps
// mistaking for coverage. What THIS fn owns is the space conversion, which nothing else in
// the fire path knows about.
// =====================================================================
pub comptime fn scene_vsplit_line(s: Scene, wy: int) -> int {
    // 15 is the lock sentinel, inlined per the pin block at the top of this file.
    ensure(s.sc_v_factor == 15,
           "scene_vsplit_line(): this scene's Plane B tracks the camera (v_factor {s.sc_v_factor}), so a layer top has no comptime SCREEN line — Vscroll_BG changes every frame and the fire line is baked. An authored scene cannot reach this (scene() refuses the combination outright); a Scene{{ .. }} literal can, and this is where it stops")
    return scene_plane_line(s, wy) - s.sc_v_offset
}

// =====================================================================
// scene_vsplit_fires — THE LOWERING (P3 Task 11): per-layer vertical depth to raster fires.
//
// Returns a FIRE LIST, built by calling `fx_vscroll_split` — the preset that already
// existed. That is the whole design: this fn owns the scene half (which layers, at which
// screen lines, in which order) and owns NOTHING about the raster half. The op, the
// command longword, the solved blanking spin, the landing check, the density guard and the
// axis-4b HInt total all belong to the DSL and are reached by calling it, so there is no
// second encoder to drift from the first. (`fx_vscroll_split` is a raster_dsl name and
// raster_dsl IS a sigil COMPTIME_HELPERS member, so it is glob-injected at every call site
// and resolves inside this body — the safe half of docs/EMP_PITFALLS.md §2.)
//
// The result composes: `compose([ scene_vsplit_fires(s), ...other presets ])` merges fires
// that land on the same line, and `patchable(scene_vsplit_fires(s), ch:, lo:, hi:)` is how a
// split would follow a world anchor rather than a static top. Neither is this fn's business.
//
// STRICTLY ASCENDING SCREEN LINES. Two splits on one line would be two whole-plane vertical
// scroll values for the same row, and `compose` would MERGE them into one fire holding both
// writes — the second silently winning. Layer tops ascend by construction (Step 4a's search
// requires it), so this can only fire on two tops with the same screen image; it names them.
//
// THE EMPTY RESULT IS REFUSED. A fold that returns [] for a scene with no vsplit is the
// silent-empty failure this tree has already paid for once (an imported bound collapsed a
// range and `compose` emitted zero fires with no diagnostic — raster_dsl.emp:449-457). A
// caller with nothing to lower should not be calling this.
// =====================================================================
pub comptime fn scene_vsplit_fires(s: Scene) -> array {
    // The one-dot rule — field, then index, then field (see scene_caps()).
    let ls = s.sc_layers
    let n  = s.sc_count
    ensure(derived_vsplit(ls, n) == 1,
           "scene_vsplit_fires(): this scene has no layer carrying vsplit: At(..), so the lowering has nothing to build and would return an EMPTY fire list. An empty program is not a program (raster_program refuses one), and a fold that quietly returns nothing is how a lost attachment stays invisible — call this only for a scene that authors a vertical split")
    comptime var out = []
    comptime var prev = -1
    for i in 0..n {
        let l  = ls[i]
        let vs = l.ly_vsplit
        if scene_vsplit_is_none(vs) == 0 {
            let wy   = l.ly_world_y
            let line = scene_vsplit_line(s, wy)
            ensure(line > prev,
                   "scene_vsplit_fires(): layer {i}'s vertical split lands on screen line {line}, which is not below the previous split's — two whole-plane vertical scroll values for one row. compose() would merge them into a single fire carrying both writes and the second would silently win. Give the two tops different screen images, or drop one split")
            prev = line
            out = out ++ fx_vscroll_split(line, scene_vsplit_offset(vs))
        }
    }
    return out
}

// scene_band — the band RECORD for layer `i`: the 10-byte legacy entry, plus the per-layer
// deform extension in a game that declares CAP_MULTI_DEFORM_TABLE (P3 Task 8, design 3.1).
// With BAND_EXT_N at 0 — both shipped games — this lowers to exactly the bytes it always
// did, which is what the section 8.1 witness pins.
//
// THE EXTENSION ARM BELOW IS DEAD IN EVERY SHIPPED GAME, and dead code is not coverage:
// nothing raises the bit until Task 9 authors `deform: own(...)`, so the only evidence the
// arm emits correctly is Task 8's spike (docs/benchmarks/scanline-p3/EXTENDED-RECORD.md,
// ROM bytes in both arms). Its reachability belongs on Task 15's poison list. The `if` is
// single-level in expression position ON PURPOSE — a NESTED if-expression yields unit
// silently (docs/EMP_PITFALLS.md section 1), which here would fold the extension array away
// with no diagnostic.
//
// P3 TASK 7 RESHAPED THE RECORD WITHOUT RESIZING IT (see parallax.emp's band_entry
// banner): the top is a u16 PLANE LINE via scene_plane_line(), and the two 1-bit factor
// op flags are packed into one `band_factor_ops` byte to pay for the extra byte.
//
// The factor split is band()'s, transcribed inline (a shared helper would be an imported
// name inside a body — see the pin block): bits 0-3 shift1, bits 4-7 shift2, bit 8 op.
// See parallax_dsl.packed() for the encoding's authority.
pub comptime fn scene_band(s: Scene, i: int) -> band_record {
    let n = s.sc_count
    ensure(i >= 0 && i < n,
           "scene_band(): layer index {i} is outside the scene's {n} real layers — the padding entries past sc_count are not lowerable")
    // The one-dot split again — see the note in scene_caps().
    let ls = s.sc_layers
    let l  = ls[i]
    let fa = l.ly_fa
    let fb = l.ly_fb
    let wy = l.ly_world_y
    // FLAT ACCUMULATOR over statement ifs — an `if` in block-tail position yields unit
    // SILENTLY (docs/EMP_PITFALLS.md §1), and this one folds a mask. Bits 0 and 1 are
    // inlined per the pin block (BAND_OP_BIT_A / BAND_OP_BIT_B).
    comptime var ops = 0
    if ((fa >> 8) & 1) != 0 {
        ops = ops | 1
    }
    if ((fb >> 8) & 1) != 0 {
        ops = ops | 2
    }
    // The curve tail's authored half, folded the same way (P3 Task 10). Bits 0 and 1 are
    // CURVE_FLAG_OP_BIT / CURVE_FLAG_ACTIVE_BIT, inlined per the pin block at the top of
    // this file; bit 2 (CONT) is written at RUNTIME by Step 4b on a split entry and is
    // never authored, so it is absent here by construction rather than cleared.
    let curve_to = scene_curve_to(l.ly_curve)
    comptime var curve_flags = 0
    if ((curve_to >> 8) & 1) != 0 {
        curve_flags = curve_flags | 1
    }
    if scene_curve_is_none(l.ly_curve) == 0 {
        curve_flags = curve_flags | 2
    }
    return band_record{
        br_base: band_entry{
            band_top_plane:      scene_plane_line(s, wy),
            band_factor_a_s1:    fa & 15,
            band_factor_a_s2:    (fa >> 4) & 15,
            band_factor_b_s1:    fb & 15,
            band_factor_b_s2:    (fb >> 4) & 15,
            band_factor_ops:     ops,
            band_deform_shift_a: l.ly_dsa,
            band_deform_shift_b: l.ly_dsb,
            band_phase_offset:   l.ly_phase,
        },
        br_ext: if BAND_EXT_N == 1 { [ band_ext{ bx_deform_table_a: band_table_a(s, l),
                                                 bx_deform_table_b: band_table_b(s, l),
                                                 bx_deform_speed_a: band_speed_a(s, l),
                                                 bx_deform_speed_b: band_speed_b(s, l) } ] } else { [] },
        // THE CURVE TAIL (P3 Task 10). Four AUTHORED bytes and three ZERO words: bc_step /
        // bc_rem / bc_span are DERIVED EVERY FRAME by the curve hoist into the SHADOW copy
        // of this record, and their ROM image is never read. Emitting them as zeros rather
        // than omitting them is what lets Step 4b's whole-entry copies carry a curve's
        // parameters to its anchored split for free — see parallax.emp's band_curve banner.
        //
        // Same single-level if-expression as br_ext above, and for the same reason: a
        // NESTED if-expression yields unit silently (docs/EMP_PITFALLS.md §1), which here
        // would fold the tail away with no diagnostic.
        br_curve: if BAND_CURVE_N == 1 { [ band_curve{ bc_to_s1: curve_to & 15,
                                                       bc_to_s2: (curve_to >> 4) & 15,
                                                       bc_flags: curve_flags,
                                                       bc_pad:   0,
                                                       bc_step:  0,
                                                       bc_rem:   0,
                                                       bc_span:  0 } ] } else { [] },
    }
}

// =====================================================================
// THE EXTENSION'S FOUR FIELDS — INHERITANCE RESOLVED AT COMPTIME (P3 Task 9).
//
// band_ext's fields used to be documented as "0 = inherit pcfg_deform_table_fg". They
// are not: the LOWERING resolves the inheritance and every emitted extension carries the
// EFFECTIVE table and speed for that band. The runtime therefore loads two pointers per
// band and tests neither.
//
// WHY THE RESOLUTION IS HERE AND NOT IN THE FILL LOOP. Both inputs are comptime
// constants — the scene's plane attachment and the layer's own — so a runtime `tst`/`beq`
// pair per band per plane would spend the frame's hot path re-deciding something the
// build knows, which is the rule this codebase opens with. It also keeps the gated block
// in Parallax_Fill_PerLine to two instructions, which is what makes the task's model
// parameter a clean per-band constant instead of a data-dependent branch.
//
// CONSEQUENCE WORTH STATING: in a game that declares CAP_MULTI_DEFORM_TABLE, EVERY band
// carries the extension, including bands with no own() — they carry the scene's tables,
// which is what "inherit" resolved to. The extension is a record-shape property of the
// GAME, never of the band (design §3.1), and that is why the size pins are on the record.
//
// FOUR ONE-LINE FUNCTIONS, NOT ONE WITH A PLANE ARGUMENT: each is a single-level
// if-expression in return position (a NESTED one yields unit silently —
// docs/EMP_PITFALLS.md §1), and the A/B asymmetry is real, not parametric — the two
// differ only in WHICH scene-level attachment they fall back to.
// =====================================================================
pub comptime fn band_table_a(s: Scene, l: SceneLayer) -> Label {
    let d = l.ly_deform
    return if scene_deform_is_own(d) == 1 { scene_deform_table(d) } else { scene_deform_table(s.sc_deform_fg) }
}
pub comptime fn band_table_b(s: Scene, l: SceneLayer) -> Label {
    let d = l.ly_deform
    return if scene_deform_is_own(d) == 1 { scene_deform_table(d) } else { scene_deform_table(s.sc_deform_bg) }
}
pub comptime fn band_speed_a(s: Scene, l: SceneLayer) -> int {
    let d = l.ly_deform
    return if scene_deform_is_own(d) == 1 { scene_deform_speed(d) } else { scene_deform_speed(s.sc_deform_fg) }
}
pub comptime fn band_speed_b(s: Scene, l: SceneLayer) -> int {
    let d = l.ly_deform
    return if scene_deform_is_own(d) == 1 { scene_deform_speed(d) } else { scene_deform_speed(s.sc_deform_bg) }
}
